window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-06-1", "source": "2021年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay based on the graph below. You should start your essay with a brief description of the graph and comment on China's achievements in urbanization. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "As is vividly shown in the line graph above, the degree of urbanization in China rose steadily and remarkably\nover nearly four decades. Specifically, the proportion of urban population climbed from a mere 19.39% in 1980\nto a striking 60.6% in 2019, tripling within the period, with the growth becoming especially rapid after the\nyear 2000.\nBehind this rapid urbanization lie China's tremendous achievements. To begin with, decades of sustained\neconomic growth created vast job opportunities in cities, drawing hundreds of millions of rural residents into\nurban areas. Moreover, forward-looking government policies and large-scale infrastructure construction\naccelerated the transfer of population and industries from the countryside to towns. Such progress not only\nreflects the modernization of the nation but also greatly improves people's living standards and promotes\nbalanced regional development.\nIn conclusion, the impressive climb of the urbanization curve mirrors the remarkable strides China has\nmade. If this momentum is sustained wisely, the country will surely embrace an even brighter and more\nprosperous future.", "image": "img/2021-06-1.jpg"}, "translation": {"prompt": "海南是仅次于台湾的中国第二大岛，是位于中国最南端的省份。海南岛风景秀丽，气候宜人，阳光充足，生物多样，温泉密布，海水清澈，大部分海滩几乎全年都是游泳和日光浴的理想场所，因而被誉为中国的四季花园和度假胜地，每年都吸引了大批中外游客。 海南1988年建省以来，旅游业、服务业、高新技术产业飞速发展，是中国唯一的省级经济特区。在中央政府和全国人民的大力支持下，海南将建成中国最大的自由贸易试验区。", "sample": "Hainan is China's second-largest island after Taiwan, as well as the southernmost province of the country. Hainan Island boasts beautiful scenery, a pleasant climate, abundant sunshine, rich biodiversity, numerous hot springs and crystal-clear seawater, and most of its beaches are ideal places for swimming and sunbathing almost all year round. As a result, it is praised as China's garden of all seasons and a holiday resort, attracting large numbers of tourists from home and abroad every year. Since it was established as a province in 1988, Hainan's tourism, service industry and high-tech industry have developed rapidly, making it China's only provincial-level special economic zone. With the strong support of the central government and people across the country, Hainan will be built into the largest free trade pilot zone in China."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2021-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "A new study has drawn a bleak picture of cultural inclusiveness reflected in the children's literature available in Australia. Dr. Helen Adam from Edith Cowan University's School of Education  __26__  the cultural diversity of children's books. She examined the books  __27__  in the kindergarten rooms of four day-care centers in Western Australia. Just 18 percent of 2,413 books in the total collection contained any  __28__  of non-white people. Minority cultures were often featured in stereotypical or tokenistic ways, for example, by  __29__  Asian culture with chopsticks and traditional dress. Characters that did represent a minority culture usually had  __30__  roles in the books. The main characters were mostly Caucasian. This causes concern as it can lead to an impression that whiteness is of greater value.\nDr. Adam said children formed impressions about \"difference\" and identity from a very young age. Evidence has shown they develop own-race  __31__  from as young as three months of age. The books we share with young children can be a valuable opportunity to develop children's understanding of themselves and others. Books can also allow children to see diversity. They discover both similarities and differences between themselves and others. This can help develop understanding, acceptance and  __32__  of diversity.\nCensus data has shown Australians come from more than 200 countries. They speak over 300 languages at home. Additionally, Australians belong to more than 100 different religious groups. They also work in more than 1,000 different occupations. \"Australia is a multicultural society. The current  __33__  promotion of white middle-class ideas and lifestyles risks  __34__  children from minority groups. This can give white middle-class children a sense of  __35__  or privilege,\" Dr. Adam said.", "matchIntro": "How Marconi Gave Us the Wireless World", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "A hundred years before iconic figures like Bill Gates and Steve Jobs permeated our lives, an Irish-Italian inventor laid the foundation of the communication explosion of the 21st century. Guglielmo Marconi was arguably the first truly global figure in modern communication. Not only was he the first to communicate globally, he was the first to think globally about communication. Marconi may not have been the greatest inventor of his time, but more than anyone else, he brought about a fundamental shift in the way we communicate."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Today's globally networked media and communication system has its origins in the 19th century, when, for the first time, messages were sent electronically across great distances. The telegraph, the telephone, and radio were the obvious predecessors of the Internet, iPods, and mobile phones. What made the link from then to now was the development of wireless communication. Marconi was the first to develop and perfect this system, using the recently-discovered \"air waves\" that make up the electromagnetic spectrum."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Between 1896, when he applied for his first patent in England at the age of 22, and his death in Italy in 1937, Marconi was at the center of every major innovation in electronic communication. He was also a skilled and sophisticated organizer, an entrepreneurial innovator, who mastered the use of corporate strategy, media relations, government lobbying, international diplomacy, patents, and prosecution. Marconi was really interested in only one thing: the extension of mobile, personal, long-distance communication to the ends of the earth (and beyond, if we can believe some reports). Some like to refer to him as a genius, but if there was any genius to Marconi it was this vision."}, {"label": "D", "text": "In 1901 he succeeded in signaling across the Atlantic, from the west coast of England to Newfoundland in the USA, despite the claims of science that it could not be done. In 1924 he convinced the British government to encircle the world with a chain of wireless stations using the latest technology that he had devised, shortwave radio. There are some who say Marconi lost his edge when commercial broadcasting came along; he didn't see that radio could or should be used to frivolous(无聊的) ends. In one of his last public speeches, a radio broadcast to the United States in March 1937, he deplored that broadcasting had become a one-way means of communication and foresaw it moving in another direction, toward communication as a means of exchange. That was visionary genius."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Marconi's career was devoted to making wireless communication happen cheaply, efficiently, smoothly, and with an elegance that would appear to be intuitive and uncomplicated to the user-friendly, if you will. There is a direct connection from Marconi to today's social media, search engines, and program streaming that can best be summed up by an admittedly provocative exclamation: the 20th century did not exist. In a sense, Marconi's vision jumped from his time to our own."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Marconi invented the idea of global communication—or, more straightforwardly, globally networked, mobile, wireless communication. Initially, this was wireless Morse code telegraphy(电报通讯), the principal communication technology of his day. Marconi was the first to develop a practical method for wireless telegraphy using radio waves. He borrowed technical details from many sources, but what set him apart was a self-confident vision of the power of communication technology on the one hand, and, on the other, of the steps that needed to be taken to consolidate his own position as a player in that field. Tracing Marconi's lifeline leads us into the story of modern communication itself. There were other important figures, but Marconi towered over them all in reach, power, and influence, as well as in the grip he had on the popular imagination of his time. Marconi was quite simply the central figure in the emergence of a modern understanding of communication."}, {"label": "G", "text": "In his lifetime, Marconi foresaw the development of television and the fax machine, GPS, radar, and the portable hand-held telephone. Two months before he died, newspapers were reporting that he was working on a \"death ray,\" and that he had \"killed a rat with an intricate device at a distance of three feet.\" By then, anything Marconi said or did was newsworthy. Stock prices rose or sank according to his pronouncements. If Marconi said he thought it might rain, there was likely to be a run on umbrellas."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Marconi's biography is also a story about choices and the motivations behind them. At one level, Marconi could be fiercely autonomous and independent of the constraints of his own social class. On another scale, he was a perpetual outsider. Wherever he went, he was never \"of\" the group; he was always the \"other,\" considered foreign in Britain, British in Italy, and \"not American\" in the United States. At the same time, he also suffered tremendously from a need for acceptance that drove, and sometimes stained, every one of his relationships."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Marconi placed a permanent stamp on the way we live. He was the first person to imagine a practical application for the wireless spectrum, and to develop it successfully into a global communication system—in both terms of the word; that is, worldwide and all-inclusive. He was able to do this because of a combination of factors—most important, timing and opportunity—but the single-mindedness and determination with which he carried out his self-imposed mission was fundamentally character-based; millions of Marconi's contemporaries had the same class, gender, race, and colonial privilege as he, but only a handful did anything with it. Marconi needed to achieve the goal that was set in his mind as an adolescent; by the time he reached adulthood, he understood, intuitively, that in order to have an impact he had to both develop an independent economic base and align himself with political power. Disciplined, uncritical loyalty to political power became his compass for the choices he had to make."}, {"label": "J", "text": "At the same time, Marconi was uncompromisingly independent intellectually. Shortly after Marconi's death, the nuclear physicist Enrico Fermi—soon to be the developer of the Manhattan Project—wrote that Marconi proved that theory and experimentation were complementary features of progress. \"Experience can rarely, unless guided by a theoretical concept, arrive at results of any great significance…on the other hand, an excessive trust in theoretical conviction would have prevented Marconi from persisting in experiments which were destined to bring about a revolution in the technique of radio-communications.\" In other words, Marconi had the advantage of not being burdened by preconceived assumptions."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The most controversial aspect of Marconi's life—and the reason why there has been no satisfying biography of Marconi until now—was his uncritical embrace of Benito Mussolini. At first this was not problematic for him. But as the regressive(倒退的) nature of Mussolini's regime became clear, he began to suffer a crisis of conscience. However, after a lifetime of moving within the circles of power, he was unable to break with authority, and served Mussolini faithfully (as president of Italy's national research council and royal academy, as well as a member of the Fascist Grand Council) until the day he died—conveniently—in 1937, shortly before he would have had to take a stand in the conflict that consumed a world that he had, in part, created."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Humans are fascinated by the source of their failings and virtues: This preoccupation inevitably leads to an old debate: whether nature or nurture moulds us more. A revolution in genetics has poised this as a modern political question about the character of our society: if personalities are hard-wired into our genes, what can governments do to help us? It feels morally questionable, yet claims of genetic selection by intelligence are making headlines.\nThis is down to \"hereditarian\"(遗传论的) science and a recent paper claimed \"differences in exam performance between pupils attending selective and non-selective schools mirror the genetic differences between them\". With such an assertion, the work was predictably greeted by a lot of absurd claims about \"genetics determining academic success\". What the research revealed was the rather less surprising result: the educational benefits of selective schools largely disappear once pupils' inborn ability and socio-economic background were taken into account. It is a glimpse of the blindingly obvious—and there's nothing to back strongly either a hereditary or environmental argument.\nYet the paper does say children are \"unintentionally genetically selected\" by the school system. Central to hereditarian science is a tall claim: that identifiable variations in genetic sequences can predict an individual's aptness to learn, reason and solve problems. This is problematic on many levels. A teacher could not seriously tell a parent their child has a low genetic tendency to study when external factors clearly exist. Unlike-minded academics say the inheritability of human traits is scientifically unsound. At best there is a weak statistical association and not a causal link between DNA and intelligence. Yet sophisticated statistics are used to create an intimidatory atmosphere of scientific certainty.\nWhile there's an undoubted genetic basis to individual difference, it is wrong to think that socially defined groups can be genetically accounted for. The fixation on genes as destiny is surely false too. Medical predictability can rarely be based on DNA alone; the environment matters too. Something as complex as intellect is likely to be affected by many factors beyond genes. If hereditarians want to advance their cause it will require more balanced interpretation and not just acts of advocacy.\nGenetic selection is a way of exerting influence over others, \"the ultimate collective control of human destinies,\" as writer H. G. Wells put it. Knowledge becomes power and power requires a sense of responsibility. In understanding cognitive ability, we must not elevate discrimination to a science, allowing people to climb the ladder of life only as far as their cells might suggest. This will need a more sceptical eye on the science. As technology progresses, we all have a duty to make sure that we shape a future that we would want to find ourselves in.", "47": "Humans are fascinated by the source of their failings and virtues: This preoccupation inevitably leads to an old debate: whether nature or nurture moulds us more. A revolution in genetics has poised this as a modern political question about the character of our society: if personalities are hard-wired into our genes, what can governments do to help us? It feels morally questionable, yet claims of genetic selection by intelligence are making headlines.\nThis is down to \"hereditarian\"(遗传论的) science and a recent paper claimed \"differences in exam performance between pupils attending selective and non-selective schools mirror the genetic differences between them\". With such an assertion, the work was predictably greeted by a lot of absurd claims about \"genetics determining academic success\". What the research revealed was the rather less surprising result: the educational benefits of selective schools largely disappear once pupils' inborn ability and socio-economic background were taken into account. It is a glimpse of the blindingly obvious—and there's nothing to back strongly either a hereditary or environmental argument.\nYet the paper does say children are \"unintentionally genetically selected\" by the school system. Central to hereditarian science is a tall claim: that identifiable variations in genetic sequences can predict an individual's aptness to learn, reason and solve problems. This is problematic on many levels. A teacher could not seriously tell a parent their child has a low genetic tendency to study when external factors clearly exist. Unlike-minded academics say the inheritability of human traits is scientifically unsound. At best there is a weak statistical association and not a causal link between DNA and intelligence. Yet sophisticated statistics are used to create an intimidatory atmosphere of scientific certainty.\nWhile there's an undoubted genetic basis to individual difference, it is wrong to think that socially defined groups can be genetically accounted for. The fixation on genes as destiny is surely false too. Medical predictability can rarely be based on DNA alone; the environment matters too. Something as complex as intellect is likely to be affected by many factors beyond genes. If hereditarians want to advance their cause it will require more balanced interpretation and not just acts of advocacy.\nGenetic selection is a way of exerting influence over others, \"the ultimate collective control of human destinies,\" as writer H. G. Wells put it. Knowledge becomes power and power requires a sense of responsibility. In understanding cognitive ability, we must not elevate discrimination to a science, allowing people to climb the ladder of life only as far as their cells might suggest. This will need a more sceptical eye on the science. As technology progresses, we all have a duty to make sure that we shape a future that we would want to find ourselves in.", "48": "Humans are fascinated by the source of their failings and virtues: This preoccupation inevitably leads to an old debate: whether nature or nurture moulds us more. A revolution in genetics has poised this as a modern political question about the character of our society: if personalities are hard-wired into our genes, what can governments do to help us? It feels morally questionable, yet claims of genetic selection by intelligence are making headlines.\nThis is down to \"hereditarian\"(遗传论的) science and a recent paper claimed \"differences in exam performance between pupils attending selective and non-selective schools mirror the genetic differences between them\". With such an assertion, the work was predictably greeted by a lot of absurd claims about \"genetics determining academic success\". What the research revealed was the rather less surprising result: the educational benefits of selective schools largely disappear once pupils' inborn ability and socio-economic background were taken into account. It is a glimpse of the blindingly obvious—and there's nothing to back strongly either a hereditary or environmental argument.\nYet the paper does say children are \"unintentionally genetically selected\" by the school system. Central to hereditarian science is a tall claim: that identifiable variations in genetic sequences can predict an individual's aptness to learn, reason and solve problems. This is problematic on many levels. A teacher could not seriously tell a parent their child has a low genetic tendency to study when external factors clearly exist. Unlike-minded academics say the inheritability of human traits is scientifically unsound. At best there is a weak statistical association and not a causal link between DNA and intelligence. Yet sophisticated statistics are used to create an intimidatory atmosphere of scientific certainty.\nWhile there's an undoubted genetic basis to individual difference, it is wrong to think that socially defined groups can be genetically accounted for. The fixation on genes as destiny is surely false too. Medical predictability can rarely be based on DNA alone; the environment matters too. Something as complex as intellect is likely to be affected by many factors beyond genes. If hereditarians want to advance their cause it will require more balanced interpretation and not just acts of advocacy.\nGenetic selection is a way of exerting influence over others, \"the ultimate collective control of human destinies,\" as writer H. G. Wells put it. Knowledge becomes power and power requires a sense of responsibility. In understanding cognitive ability, we must not elevate discrimination to a science, allowing people to climb the ladder of life only as far as their cells might suggest. This will need a more sceptical eye on the science. As technology progresses, we all have a duty to make sure that we shape a future that we would want to find ourselves in.", "49": "Humans are fascinated by the source of their failings and virtues: This preoccupation inevitably leads to an old debate: whether nature or nurture moulds us more. A revolution in genetics has poised this as a modern political question about the character of our society: if personalities are hard-wired into our genes, what can governments do to help us? It feels morally questionable, yet claims of genetic selection by intelligence are making headlines.\nThis is down to \"hereditarian\"(遗传论的) science and a recent paper claimed \"differences in exam performance between pupils attending selective and non-selective schools mirror the genetic differences between them\". With such an assertion, the work was predictably greeted by a lot of absurd claims about \"genetics determining academic success\". What the research revealed was the rather less surprising result: the educational benefits of selective schools largely disappear once pupils' inborn ability and socio-economic background were taken into account. It is a glimpse of the blindingly obvious—and there's nothing to back strongly either a hereditary or environmental argument.\nYet the paper does say children are \"unintentionally genetically selected\" by the school system. Central to hereditarian science is a tall claim: that identifiable variations in genetic sequences can predict an individual's aptness to learn, reason and solve problems. This is problematic on many levels. A teacher could not seriously tell a parent their child has a low genetic tendency to study when external factors clearly exist. Unlike-minded academics say the inheritability of human traits is scientifically unsound. At best there is a weak statistical association and not a causal link between DNA and intelligence. Yet sophisticated statistics are used to create an intimidatory atmosphere of scientific certainty.\nWhile there's an undoubted genetic basis to individual difference, it is wrong to think that socially defined groups can be genetically accounted for. The fixation on genes as destiny is surely false too. Medical predictability can rarely be based on DNA alone; the environment matters too. Something as complex as intellect is likely to be affected by many factors beyond genes. If hereditarians want to advance their cause it will require more balanced interpretation and not just acts of advocacy.\nGenetic selection is a way of exerting influence over others, \"the ultimate collective control of human destinies,\" as writer H. G. Wells put it. Knowledge becomes power and power requires a sense of responsibility. In understanding cognitive ability, we must not elevate discrimination to a science, allowing people to climb the ladder of life only as far as their cells might suggest. This will need a more sceptical eye on the science. As technology progresses, we all have a duty to make sure that we shape a future that we would want to find ourselves in.", "50": "Humans are fascinated by the source of their failings and virtues: This preoccupation inevitably leads to an old debate: whether nature or nurture moulds us more. A revolution in genetics has poised this as a modern political question about the character of our society: if personalities are hard-wired into our genes, what can governments do to help us? It feels morally questionable, yet claims of genetic selection by intelligence are making headlines.\nThis is down to \"hereditarian\"(遗传论的) science and a recent paper claimed \"differences in exam performance between pupils attending selective and non-selective schools mirror the genetic differences between them\". With such an assertion, the work was predictably greeted by a lot of absurd claims about \"genetics determining academic success\". What the research revealed was the rather less surprising result: the educational benefits of selective schools largely disappear once pupils' inborn ability and socio-economic background were taken into account. It is a glimpse of the blindingly obvious—and there's nothing to back strongly either a hereditary or environmental argument.\nYet the paper does say children are \"unintentionally genetically selected\" by the school system. Central to hereditarian science is a tall claim: that identifiable variations in genetic sequences can predict an individual's aptness to learn, reason and solve problems. This is problematic on many levels. A teacher could not seriously tell a parent their child has a low genetic tendency to study when external factors clearly exist. Unlike-minded academics say the inheritability of human traits is scientifically unsound. At best there is a weak statistical association and not a causal link between DNA and intelligence. Yet sophisticated statistics are used to create an intimidatory atmosphere of scientific certainty.\nWhile there's an undoubted genetic basis to individual difference, it is wrong to think that socially defined groups can be genetically accounted for. The fixation on genes as destiny is surely false too. Medical predictability can rarely be based on DNA alone; the environment matters too. Something as complex as intellect is likely to be affected by many factors beyond genes. If hereditarians want to advance their cause it will require more balanced interpretation and not just acts of advocacy.\nGenetic selection is a way of exerting influence over others, \"the ultimate collective control of human destinies,\" as writer H. G. Wells put it. Knowledge becomes power and power requires a sense of responsibility. In understanding cognitive ability, we must not elevate discrimination to a science, allowing people to climb the ladder of life only as far as their cells might suggest. This will need a more sceptical eye on the science. As technology progresses, we all have a duty to make sure that we shape a future that we would want to find ourselves in.", "51": "Nicola Sturgeon's speech last Tuesday setting out the Scottish government's legislative programme for the year ahead confirmed what was already pretty clear. Scottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors, with Edinburgh likely to lead the way.\nTourist taxes are not new. The Himalayan kingdom of Bhutan has a longstanding policy of charging visitors a daily fee. France's tax on overnight stays was introduced to assist thermal spa(温泉) towns to develop, and around half of French local authorities use it today.\nBut such levies are on the rise. Moves by Barcelona and Venice to deal with the phenomenon of \"over-tourism\" through the use of charges have recently gained prominence. Japan and Greece are among the countries to have recently introduced tourist taxes.\nThat the UK lags behind is due to our weak, by international standards, local government, as well as the opposition to taxes and regulation of our aggressively pro-market ruling party. Some UK cities have lobbied without success for the power to levy a charge on visitors. Such levies are no universal remedy as the amounts raised would be tiny compared with what has been taken away by central government since 2010. Still, it is to be hoped that the Scottish government's bold move will prompt others to act. There is no reason why visitors to the UK, or domestic tourists on holiday in hotspots such as Cornwall, should be exempt from taxation-particularly when vital local services including waste collection, park maintenance and arts and culture spending are under unprecedented strain.\nOn the contrary, compelling tourists to make a financial contribution to the places they visit beyond their personal consumption should be part of a wider cultural shift. Westerners with disposable incomes have often behaved as if they have a right to go wherever they choose with little regard for the consequences. Just as the environmental harm caused by aviation and other transport must come under far greater scrutiny, the social cost of tourism must also be confronted. This includes the impact of short-term lets on housing costs and quality of life for residents. Several European capitals, including Paris and Berlin, are leading a campaign for tougher regulation by the European Union. It also includes the impact of overcrowding, litter and the kinds of behaviour associated with noisy parties.\nThere is no \"one size fits all\" solution to this problem. The existence of new revenue streams for some but not all councils is complicated, and businesses are often opposed, fearing higher costs will make them uncompetitive. But those places that want them must be given the chance to make tourist taxes work.", "52": "Nicola Sturgeon's speech last Tuesday setting out the Scottish government's legislative programme for the year ahead confirmed what was already pretty clear. Scottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors, with Edinburgh likely to lead the way.\nTourist taxes are not new. The Himalayan kingdom of Bhutan has a longstanding policy of charging visitors a daily fee. France's tax on overnight stays was introduced to assist thermal spa(温泉) towns to develop, and around half of French local authorities use it today.\nBut such levies are on the rise. Moves by Barcelona and Venice to deal with the phenomenon of \"over-tourism\" through the use of charges have recently gained prominence. Japan and Greece are among the countries to have recently introduced tourist taxes.\nThat the UK lags behind is due to our weak, by international standards, local government, as well as the opposition to taxes and regulation of our aggressively pro-market ruling party. Some UK cities have lobbied without success for the power to levy a charge on visitors. Such levies are no universal remedy as the amounts raised would be tiny compared with what has been taken away by central government since 2010. Still, it is to be hoped that the Scottish government's bold move will prompt others to act. There is no reason why visitors to the UK, or domestic tourists on holiday in hotspots such as Cornwall, should be exempt from taxation-particularly when vital local services including waste collection, park maintenance and arts and culture spending are under unprecedented strain.\nOn the contrary, compelling tourists to make a financial contribution to the places they visit beyond their personal consumption should be part of a wider cultural shift. Westerners with disposable incomes have often behaved as if they have a right to go wherever they choose with little regard for the consequences. Just as the environmental harm caused by aviation and other transport must come under far greater scrutiny, the social cost of tourism must also be confronted. This includes the impact of short-term lets on housing costs and quality of life for residents. Several European capitals, including Paris and Berlin, are leading a campaign for tougher regulation by the European Union. It also includes the impact of overcrowding, litter and the kinds of behaviour associated with noisy parties.\nThere is no \"one size fits all\" solution to this problem. The existence of new revenue streams for some but not all councils is complicated, and businesses are often opposed, fearing higher costs will make them uncompetitive. But those places that want them must be given the chance to make tourist taxes work.", "53": "Nicola Sturgeon's speech last Tuesday setting out the Scottish government's legislative programme for the year ahead confirmed what was already pretty clear. Scottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors, with Edinburgh likely to lead the way.\nTourist taxes are not new. The Himalayan kingdom of Bhutan has a longstanding policy of charging visitors a daily fee. France's tax on overnight stays was introduced to assist thermal spa(温泉) towns to develop, and around half of French local authorities use it today.\nBut such levies are on the rise. Moves by Barcelona and Venice to deal with the phenomenon of \"over-tourism\" through the use of charges have recently gained prominence. Japan and Greece are among the countries to have recently introduced tourist taxes.\nThat the UK lags behind is due to our weak, by international standards, local government, as well as the opposition to taxes and regulation of our aggressively pro-market ruling party. Some UK cities have lobbied without success for the power to levy a charge on visitors. Such levies are no universal remedy as the amounts raised would be tiny compared with what has been taken away by central government since 2010. Still, it is to be hoped that the Scottish government's bold move will prompt others to act. There is no reason why visitors to the UK, or domestic tourists on holiday in hotspots such as Cornwall, should be exempt from taxation-particularly when vital local services including waste collection, park maintenance and arts and culture spending are under unprecedented strain.\nOn the contrary, compelling tourists to make a financial contribution to the places they visit beyond their personal consumption should be part of a wider cultural shift. Westerners with disposable incomes have often behaved as if they have a right to go wherever they choose with little regard for the consequences. Just as the environmental harm caused by aviation and other transport must come under far greater scrutiny, the social cost of tourism must also be confronted. This includes the impact of short-term lets on housing costs and quality of life for residents. Several European capitals, including Paris and Berlin, are leading a campaign for tougher regulation by the European Union. It also includes the impact of overcrowding, litter and the kinds of behaviour associated with noisy parties.\nThere is no \"one size fits all\" solution to this problem. The existence of new revenue streams for some but not all councils is complicated, and businesses are often opposed, fearing higher costs will make them uncompetitive. But those places that want them must be given the chance to make tourist taxes work.", "54": "Nicola Sturgeon's speech last Tuesday setting out the Scottish government's legislative programme for the year ahead confirmed what was already pretty clear. Scottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors, with Edinburgh likely to lead the way.\nTourist taxes are not new. The Himalayan kingdom of Bhutan has a longstanding policy of charging visitors a daily fee. France's tax on overnight stays was introduced to assist thermal spa(温泉) towns to develop, and around half of French local authorities use it today.\nBut such levies are on the rise. Moves by Barcelona and Venice to deal with the phenomenon of \"over-tourism\" through the use of charges have recently gained prominence. Japan and Greece are among the countries to have recently introduced tourist taxes.\nThat the UK lags behind is due to our weak, by international standards, local government, as well as the opposition to taxes and regulation of our aggressively pro-market ruling party. Some UK cities have lobbied without success for the power to levy a charge on visitors. Such levies are no universal remedy as the amounts raised would be tiny compared with what has been taken away by central government since 2010. Still, it is to be hoped that the Scottish government's bold move will prompt others to act. There is no reason why visitors to the UK, or domestic tourists on holiday in hotspots such as Cornwall, should be exempt from taxation-particularly when vital local services including waste collection, park maintenance and arts and culture spending are under unprecedented strain.\nOn the contrary, compelling tourists to make a financial contribution to the places they visit beyond their personal consumption should be part of a wider cultural shift. Westerners with disposable incomes have often behaved as if they have a right to go wherever they choose with little regard for the consequences. Just as the environmental harm caused by aviation and other transport must come under far greater scrutiny, the social cost of tourism must also be confronted. This includes the impact of short-term lets on housing costs and quality of life for residents. Several European capitals, including Paris and Berlin, are leading a campaign for tougher regulation by the European Union. It also includes the impact of overcrowding, litter and the kinds of behaviour associated with noisy parties.\nThere is no \"one size fits all\" solution to this problem. The existence of new revenue streams for some but not all councils is complicated, and businesses are often opposed, fearing higher costs will make them uncompetitive. But those places that want them must be given the chance to make tourist taxes work.", "55": "Nicola Sturgeon's speech last Tuesday setting out the Scottish government's legislative programme for the year ahead confirmed what was already pretty clear. Scottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors, with Edinburgh likely to lead the way.\nTourist taxes are not new. The Himalayan kingdom of Bhutan has a longstanding policy of charging visitors a daily fee. France's tax on overnight stays was introduced to assist thermal spa(温泉) towns to develop, and around half of French local authorities use it today.\nBut such levies are on the rise. Moves by Barcelona and Venice to deal with the phenomenon of \"over-tourism\" through the use of charges have recently gained prominence. Japan and Greece are among the countries to have recently introduced tourist taxes.\nThat the UK lags behind is due to our weak, by international standards, local government, as well as the opposition to taxes and regulation of our aggressively pro-market ruling party. Some UK cities have lobbied without success for the power to levy a charge on visitors. Such levies are no universal remedy as the amounts raised would be tiny compared with what has been taken away by central government since 2010. Still, it is to be hoped that the Scottish government's bold move will prompt others to act. There is no reason why visitors to the UK, or domestic tourists on holiday in hotspots such as Cornwall, should be exempt from taxation-particularly when vital local services including waste collection, park maintenance and arts and culture spending are under unprecedented strain.\nOn the contrary, compelling tourists to make a financial contribution to the places they visit beyond their personal consumption should be part of a wider cultural shift. Westerners with disposable incomes have often behaved as if they have a right to go wherever they choose with little regard for the consequences. Just as the environmental harm caused by aviation and other transport must come under far greater scrutiny, the social cost of tourism must also be confronted. This includes the impact of short-term lets on housing costs and quality of life for residents. Several European capitals, including Paris and Berlin, are leading a campaign for tougher regulation by the European Union. It also includes the impact of overcrowding, litter and the kinds of behaviour associated with noisy parties.\nThere is no \"one size fits all\" solution to this problem. The existence of new revenue streams for some but not all councils is complicated, and businesses are often opposed, fearing higher costs will make them uncompetitive. But those places that want them must be given the chance to make tourist taxes work."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn about the man from the conversation?", "options": ["A) He will tell the management how he really feels.", "B) He will meet his new manager in two weeks.", "C) He is going to attend a job interview.", "D) He is going to leave his present job."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He is going to leave his present job.\n【定位】I resigned because I've been unhappy at that company for a long time.\n我辞职了，因为我在那家公司已经不开心很久了。\n【信号】被问者自述 I resigned 的直接陈述。男士开口第一段就说 It's my last day at work\ntomorrow. I'll start my new job in two weeks.，到第三段又点明 I resigned because\nI've been unhappy at that company for a long time。『resigned』『last day』『new\njob』几个自述合起来，明确他要离开现职——这是说话人自己交代现状的直接信号。\n【替换】I resigned ↔ leave his present job\nresign 等于 leave the job\nIt's my last day at work tomorrow. ↔ leave his present job\n明天最后一天即将离职\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了 tell them how I really feel 这串原词，但男士说的是 I'm not sure if I should\ntell them how I really feel（不确定该不该说），是犹豫，不是『他将会说』。把『不确定\n要不要』听成『他会』，正中圈套。\n借用原文：I'm not sure if I should tell them how I really feel\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项偷用了 in two weeks 这个时间词，但原文是 I'll start my new job in two weeks（两\n周后开始新工作），根本没提『见新经理』。听到 two weeks 就选 B，是被数字原词牵着\n走。\n借用原文：I'll start my new job in two weeks\nC) 偷换概念\n选项借了原文反复出现的 interview 一词，但原文的 conduct an interview with me\nbefore I leave 和 an exit interview 是『离职面谈』，而非 a job interview（求职面\n试）。同一个 interview，性质完全不同。\n借用原文：an exit interview", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the woman think about the information gained from an exit interview?", "options": ["A) It should be kept private.", "B) It should be carefully analyzed.", "C) It can be quite useful to senior managers.", "D) It can improve interviewees' job prospects."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It should be kept private.\n【定位】An exit interview is supposed to be private, but often isn't. In my company, the information\ngained from these interviews is often not confidential.\n离职面谈本应是保密的，但常常并非如此。在我公司，这些面谈得到的信息往往并不保密。\n【信号】supposed to be 表『本应当』的主张。女士说 An exit interview is supposed to be\nprivate, but often isn't，be supposed to 表示『理应、本该』，正是她对这信息该如何的\n主张：本该保密。后半 but often isn't 是补充现实，但她持的立场仍是『应当保密』。\n【替换】supposed to be private ↔ It should be kept private\nsupposed to be 等于 should be\n【排除】B) 未提及\n选项 carefully analyzed 在录音里找不到对应说法，女士谈的是保密与被滥用，没有提到\n『仔细分析』。这是凭空添加的内容。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项盗用了 senior managers 这个原词，但原文是 traded among senior managers（在\n高管间被当把柄交易），是负面滥用，而非 useful to senior managers（对高管有用）这\n种正面评价。听到 senior managers 就选，正中陷阱。\n借用原文：can be traded among senior managers\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 improve interviewees' job prospects（改善求职前景）与原文方向相反：录音第8段\n说乱说话会 injuring your future career prospects（损害前途），是变坏不是变好。极性\n被反转了。\n借用原文：injuring your future career prospects", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "Why does the man want to rethink what he will say in the coming exit interview?", "options": ["A) It may leave a negative impression on the interviewer.", "B) It may adversely affect his future career prospects.", "C) It may displease his immediate superiors.", "D) It may do harm to his fellow employees."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It may adversely affect his future career prospects.\n【定位】There is always a chance that it could affect my reputation and my ability to network in\nthe industry.\n总有一种可能，它会影响我的声誉和我在行业里建立人脉的能力。\n【信号】it could affect 表担忧的因果句。男士说 Now you've got me rethinking what I'll\ndisclose，紧接 There is always a chance that it could affect my reputation and my\nability to network in the industry。could affect 点出他改主意的原因——怕影响声誉和\n人脉；下一段女士又用 injuring your future career prospects 把这归结为『损害未来职业\n前景』，因果信号明确。\n【替换】affect my reputation and my ability to network in the industry ↔\nadversely affect his future career prospects\n声誉人脉受损概括为前途受损\ninjuring your future career prospects ↔ adversely affect his future career prospects\ninjure 等于 adversely affect\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 negative impression on the interviewer 借了话题里的 interview 氛围，但男士担心\n的是声誉和人脉、行业里的名声，从没提『给面试官留下负面印象』。这是没有依据的添\n加。\nC) 张冠李戴\n选项盗用了第6段 dirt against another manager 的 manager 语境，但那是女士讲『信息\n被用来对付别的经理』，并非男士改主意是怕惹自己的顶头上司。把别处的内容嫁接到本题\n原因上，是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：used as dirt against another manager\nD) 未提及\n选项 harm to his fellow employees 在录音里找不到对应：男士担心的是自己的前途，女\n士谈的是信息被高管滥用，谁都没说会伤到同事。这是凭空捏造的受害对象。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man think he had better do?", "options": ["A) Prepare a comprehensive exit report.", "B) Do some practice for the exit interview.", "C) Network with his close friends to find a better employer.", "D) Pour out his frustrations on a rate-your-employer website."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Pour out his frustrations on a rate-your-employer website.\n【定位】Perhaps it would be better getting things off my chest by going to one of those rate-your-\nemployer websites.\n也许更好的办法是去那些给雇主打分的网站上把心里话一吐为快。\n【信号】Perhaps it would be better 表打算的建议句。对话临近结尾，男士说 Perhaps it would\nbe better getting things off my chest by going to one of those rate-your-employer\nwebsites。it would be better 直接表达他认为更好的做法，正是题干『had better do』\n要找的打算句，出现在对话后半段，符合建议常落尾部的规律。\n【替换】getting things off my chest ↔ Pour out his frustrations\nget off chest 等于 pour out frustrations\ngoing to one of those rate-your-employer websites ↔ on a rate-your-employer website\n同一渠道名几乎原样对应\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 Prepare a comprehensive exit report 借了话题里的 exit 一词，造出『写一份详尽的\n离职报告』，但录音里男士从没说要写报告。这是凭空添加的方案。\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 Do some practice for the exit interview 盗用了 exit interview 这一原词，但全程没\n有任何人提到『为面谈做练习』；女士最后反而说 don't do the interview at all（干脆别\n做这面谈）。听到 exit interview 就选，正中原词陷阱。\n借用原文：don't do the interview at all\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Network with his close friends to find a better employer 借了第7段 network in\nthe industry 的 network 一词，但原文的 network 是『在行业建人脉』且是怕受损，并非\n『找好友另找雇主』。盗 network 来拼错意，是原词陷阱。\n借用原文：my ability to network in the industry", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What does the man want Jane Forster to talk about?", "options": ["A) Her unsuccessful journey.", "B) Her month-long expedition.", "C) Her latest documentary.", "D) Her career as a botanist."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Her latest documentary.\n【定位】Recently, Jane, you've become quite a celebrity since the release of your latest documentary.\nCan you tell us a little about it?\n简，最近你随着最新纪录片的发行成了名人。你能跟我们聊聊它吗？\n【信号】主持人的直接请求句。信号是主持人发出的请求 Can you tell us a little about it?，其中 it\n回指上一句的 your latest documentary——一问一请求，正是他想让她谈的对象，答案就\n落在这句。\n【替换】your latest documentary ↔ Her latest documentary.\n近乎原词对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nunsuccessful journey 借了对话后段主持人那句 was the journey unsuccessful? 的原词，\n但那是结尾另一问，并非开场主持人请她谈的话题，听到 unsuccessful/journey 就选属于\n答非所问。\n借用原文：was the journey unsuccessful?\nB) 原词陷阱\nmonth-long expedition 借了她回答里 my expedition 与 planned to be there for a\nmonth 的原词拼成，但主持人开场请她谈的是纪录片本身，不是\"为期一月的远征\"这件事，\n属答非所问。\n借用原文：my expedition\nD) 原词陷阱\ncareer as a botanist 借了开场 the renowned botanist 的原词，但主持人没有请她谈\"植\n物学家的职业生涯\"，只请她谈纪录片，听到 botanist 就选属答非所问。\n借用原文：the renowned botanist", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "Why does the woman describe her experience as \"far from glamorous\"?", "options": ["A) She had to live like a vegetarian.", "B) She was caught in a hurricane.", "C) She had to endure many hardships.", "D) She suffered from water shortage."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） She had to endure many hardships.\n【定位】It was far from glamorous, to be honest. The area we visited was accessible only by canoe\nand the living conditions in the hut were primitive, to say the least. There was no electricity.\nAnd our water supply was a nearby stream.\n老实说，那远谈不上光鲜。我们去的地方只能靠独木舟进入，小屋里的居住条件说轻点也是\n非常原始的。没有电。我们的水源就是附近一条小溪。\n【信号】原话之后的连串列举。信号是她说完 far from glamorous 后一口气列举多重恶劣条件：\naccessible only by canoe、living conditions in the hut were primitive、no\nelectricity、water supply was a nearby stream。连珠炮式的多项困难，正是答案句。\n【替换】the living conditions in the hut were primitive ↔ She had to endure many hardships.\n多项困难概括为吃苦\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nlive like a vegetarian（像素食者一样生活）极易与录音里 the vegetation indigenous to\nthe rainforest 的 vegetation 听混，但 vegetation 是\"植被\"、vegetarian 是\"素食者\"，原\n文从未说她被迫吃素，属听音致误的相近词陷阱。\n借用原文：the vegetation indigenous to the rainforest\nB) 原词陷阱\ncaught in a hurricane 借了后文 a hurricane was approaching 的原词，但飓风是导致提\n前结束（第 7 题）的原因，并非她说 far from glamorous 的缘由，张冠李戴到本题。\n借用原文：a hurricane was approaching\nD) 偷换概念\nwater shortage（缺水）盗用了 our water supply was a nearby stream 里的 water，但\n原文是说\"水源是条小溪\"（条件简陋），并非缺水；且只取一样困难，与\"many\nhardships\"的总括不符。\n借用原文：our water supply was a nearby stream", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Why did the woman and those who went with her end their trip halfway?", "options": ["A) A hurricane was coming.", "B) A flood was approaching.", "C) They had no more food in the canoe.", "D) They could no longer bear the humidity."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） A hurricane was coming.\n【定位】Halfway through the trip, we received news that a hurricane was approaching. And we had to\nevacuate at very short notice.\n行程过半时，我们收到飓风正在逼近的消息。我们不得不在极短时间内撤离。\n【信号】received news that + had to 因果链。信号是 we received news that a hurricane was\napproaching 接着 we had to evacuate——\"得知飓风逼近\"是因、\"被迫撤离\"是果，had\nto 的被迫语气坐实了这就是提前结束的原因句。\n【替换】a hurricane was approaching ↔ A hurricane was coming.\napproaching 同义于 coming\n【排除】B) 未提及\nflood（洪水）在录音里从未出现，它借了后文 river rapids（激流）造成的\"和水有关\"的联\n想，但原文撤离原因是飓风不是洪水，属凭联想编造的未提及项。\nC) 原词陷阱\nno more food in the canoe 借了 accessible only by canoe 的 canoe，但原文从没提食\n物耗尽，独木舟是\"进出方式\"而非\"断粮地点\"，听到 canoe 就选属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：accessible only by canoe\nD) 原词陷阱\nno longer bear the humidity 借了 the humidity was almost unbearable 的 humidity，\n但潮湿是描述天气难受（第 6/8 题背景），不是中途撤离的原因——撤离是因飓风，张冠李\n戴到本题。\n借用原文：the humidity was almost unbearable", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman think of the journey?", "options": ["A) It was memorable.", "B) It was unbearable.", "C) It was uneventful.", "D) It was fruitful."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） It was fruitful.\n【定位】Absolutely not. We gathered a massive amount of data about the local plant life.\n绝对不是。我们采集了关于当地植物的海量数据。\n【信号】强烈否定 + 反转后的正向定调。信号是主持人问 was the journey unsuccessful?（负面设\n问），她以 Absolutely not. 强烈否定反转，再用 gathered a massive amount of data 给\n出正向定调——\"不是失败、收获巨大\"才是她的真实态度。\n【替换】We gathered a massive amount of data ↔ It was fruitful.\n采集海量数据等于有收获\n【排除】A) 未提及\nmemorable（难忘）听上去是正面评价，像能套上整段经历，但她对行程的总评落点是\"有\n收获\"，原文并未说\"难忘\"，属凭褒义氛围误推、原文未提及的态度。\nB) 与原文相反\nunbearable 盗自 the humidity was almost unbearable，那是形容潮湿天气难忍，并非\n她对整段 journey 的评价；况且她明确否定行程失败，态度是正面，与 unbearable 相反。\n借用原文：the humidity was almost unbearable\nC) 与原文相反\nuneventful（平淡无事）与原文截然相反——她经历了飓风逼近、紧急撤离、激流行船，行\n程绝非平淡，这是把惊险经历反说成平淡的反向干扰。\n借用原文：It was frightening.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the passage say about the use of jargon terms by experts?", "options": ["A) It diminishes laymen's interest in science.", "B) It ensures the accuracy of their arguments.", "C) It makes their expressions more explicit.", "D) It hurts laymen's dignity and self-esteem."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It diminishes laymen's interest in science.\n【定位】Most of them don't realize the harmful effects of this practice. In a new study, people exposed\nto jargon when reading about subjects like autonomous vehicles and surgical robots later said\nthey were less interested in science than others who read about the same topics, but without\nthe use of specialized terms.\n他们大多数人没意识到这种做法的有害影响。在一项新研究中，阅读自动驾驶汽车、手术机\n器人等话题时接触到术语的人，事后表示自己对科学的兴趣低于那些读同样话题但没用专业\n术语的人。\n【信号】定性词harmful＋对比。答案句的信号是定性词 the harmful effects（有害影响）和紧随其\n后的对比结果 less interested in science：录音先给出\"有害\"的定性，再用接触术语者 vs\n未接触者的对照坐实是哪种害处，这两处合起来就是答案落点。\n【替换】less interested in science ↔ diminishes laymen's interest in science\n形容词改写为动词\npeople exposed to jargon ↔ laymen's interest\npeople＝laymen\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 盗用了术语话题里反复出现的 science 一词，编出 ensures the accuracy of their\narguments.（确保论点准确）。但原文只说术语\"有害\"、让人兴趣下降，从未说它\"确保准\n确\"，方向正相反，是听到 science 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：less interested in science\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说术语 makes their expressions more explicit.（使表达更清晰），是正面作用；但原\n文定性是 the harmful effects，且第 2 段说术语 sends a discouraging message，方向\n与 C 完全相反，属与原文相反的干扰。\n借用原文：the harmful effects of this practice\nD) 未提及\nD 说术语 hurts laymen's dignity and self-esteem.（伤害外行尊严与自尊）。原文确实讲\n了多种负面后果——兴趣下降、感觉 less informed、less qualified to discuss，但从未\n提\"尊严/自尊\"，dignity、self-esteem 是原文没出现的概念，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What do researchers find about people reading scientific articles containing jargon terms?", "options": ["A) They can learn to communicate with scientists.", "B) They tend to disbelieve the actual science.", "C) They feel great respect towards scientists.", "D) They will see the complexity of science."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They tend to disbelieve the actual science.\n【定位】In another study, researchers found that reading scientific articles containing jargon led\npeople to doubt the actual science. They found the opposite when a text is easier to read.\n在另一项研究中，研究者发现，阅读含有术语的科学文章会让人怀疑真实的科学。而当文章\n更易读时，他们发现的结果正相反。\n【信号】研究发现动词＋后续转折。信号是 researchers found that（研究者发现）这个直接报告发\n现的动词，题干 find 与它对应；其后还有 They found the opposite when a text is\neasier to read. 的对比，进一步把\"含术语→怀疑\"这一发现衬得更实。听到 researchers\nfound 就锁定这句。\n【替换】led people to doubt the actual science ↔ They tend to disbelieve the actual science.\ndoubt＝disbelieve\nreading scientific articles containing jargon led people ↔ They\npeople＝They\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 说他们 can learn to communicate with scientists.，借用了全篇关于\"沟通\"的话题氛围\n（如 communicating with laymen），但原文讲的是读者\"怀疑科学\"，从没说读者会\"学会\n与科学家沟通\"，属答非所问。\n借用原文：communicating with laymen\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说他们 feel great respect towards scientists.（对科学家更敬重），是正面反应；但原\n文是 doubt the actual science（怀疑科学），方向相反。读术语文章让人更不信、而非更\n敬重，属与原文相反。\n借用原文：led people to doubt the actual science\nD) 过度推断\nD 说他们 will see the complexity of science.（会看到科学的复杂），蹭了第 4 段\ncomplex science subjects 的 complex 一词，听着\"合理\"。但答案句说的是\"怀疑科学\"，\n不是\"看到科学复杂\"，原文未就读者得出\"看到复杂性\"这一发现，属过度推断。\n借用原文：complex science subjects", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does Schulman suggest scientists do when communicating with the general public?", "options": ["A) Find appropriate topics.", "B) Stimulate their interest.", "C) Explain all the jargon terms.", "D) Do away with jargon terms."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Do away with jargon terms.\n【定位】But scientists who want to communicate with the general public need to modify their\nlanguage. They need to eliminate jargon.\n但想与普通大众沟通的科学家需要调整自己的语言。他们需要去掉术语。\n【信号】转折But＋建议need to。双重信号：先是转折 But（前句说术语对专业听众尚可，But 后\n才是对大众的真正主张），再是建议动词 need to eliminate jargon。转折后＋need to 之\n处，正是对策题答案落点。\n【替换】eliminate jargon ↔ Do away with jargon terms.\neliminate＝do away with\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说要 Find appropriate topics.（找合适话题）。原文给的建议是\"去掉术语\"，全篇没\n谈\"挑选话题\"，topics 虽在前文出现过（如 read about the same topics），但从未作为\n给科学家的建议，属未提及。\nB) 答非所问\nB 说要 Stimulate their interest.（激发兴趣）。原文确实提到术语让人 less interested in\nscience，但 Schulman 的具体建议是\"去掉术语\"，并未把\"激发兴趣\"列为要做的动作，B\n是顺着\"兴趣\"话题编的笼统干扰。\n借用原文：less interested in science\nC) 与原文相反\nC 说要 Explain all the jargon terms.（解释所有术语），盗用了原文 jargon 一词，且第 1\n段还专门说过 it made no difference if the jargon terms were defined——解释/定义术\n语根本无效。建议是\"去掉\"而非\"解释\"，C 正好取了原文否定掉的做法，是与原文相反。\n借用原文：it made no difference if the jargon terms were defined in the text", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What did Texas businessman Pattillo Higgins believe?", "options": ["A) The local gassy hill might start a huge fire.", "B) There was oil leakage along the Gulf Coast.", "C) The erupting gas might endanger local children.", "D) There were oil deposits below a local gassy hill."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） There were oil deposits below a local gassy hill.\n【定位】Pattillo Higgins, a disreputable local businessman, became convinced that there was oil below\nthe gassy hill.\n帕蒂洛·希金斯，一个名声不佳的当地商人，开始确信那座漏气的山下面有石油。\n【信号】信号词 became convinced 直接交代所信。录音里 became convinced that there was\noil below the gassy hill 中的 became convinced（确信）正是表达人物信念的标志词，\n题干问 believe，听到 convinced 这类同义表态词就锁定这句。\n【替换】there was oil below the gassy hill ↔ There were oil deposits below a local gassy hill\noil 扩写为 oil deposits，句意不变\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借了第一段 schoolboys would sometimes set the hill on fire 的失火画面，但那是描\n述男孩点火，不是 Higgins 的信念，问的是他相信什么，答非所问。\n借用原文：schoolboys would sometimes set the hill on fire\nB) 偷换概念\nB 说 oil leakage（漏油）发生在海湾沿岸，但录音是 Higgins 确信油 below the gassy hill\n（埋在山下），是埋藏不是泄漏；且漏出来的是气不是油，对象与状态都改了。\n借用原文：there was oil below the gassy hill\nC) 过度推断\nC 说 erupting gas 可能危及当地儿童，录音第一段只提到 schoolboys would sometimes\nset the hill on fire，是男孩主动点火，从没说气体会危及他们，属无中生有。\n借用原文：schoolboys would sometimes set the hill on fire", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What prevented Higgins' workers from digging a proper hole to get the oil?", "options": ["A) The massive gas underground.", "B) Their lack of the needed skill.", "C) The sand under the hill.", "D) Their lack of suitable tools."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） The sand under the hill.\n【定位】The sand under the hill defeated several attempts by Higgins' workers to make a proper hole.\n山下的沙子让希金斯的工人们好几次想挖出一个像样的洞都以失败告终。\n【信号】信号词 defeated 标出受阻原因。The sand under the hill defeated several attempts 里\ndefeated（挫败）正对题干的 prevented，主语 The sand under the hill 就是那个让挖洞\n失败的障碍，听到表挫败、阻挡的动词就锁其主语。\n【替换】The sand under the hill ↔ The sand under the hill.\n原句主语原样搬入选项\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 The massive gas underground.，录音确实反复讲 gas（漏气的山），但让挖洞失败\n的是 sand 不是 gas，气体只是山的特征，盗用耳熟的 gas 来骗选。\n借用原文：gas leakage was so noticeable\nB) 未提及\nB 说工人 lack of the needed skill（缺技术），录音只说沙子让他们多次失败，从没说是工\n人技术不行，把客观障碍换成人为不足，属无中生有。\nD) 未提及\nD 说 lack of suitable tools（缺合适工具），录音只字未提工具问题，障碍始终是山下的沙\n子，同样是凭想象补出的人为原因。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the passage say about Captain Lucas' drilling method?", "options": ["A) It rendered many oil workers jobless.", "B) It was not as effective as he claimed.", "C) It gave birth to the oil drilling industry.", "D) It was not popularized until years later."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It gave birth to the oil drilling industry.\n【定位】After encountering several setbacks, Captain Lucas decided to use a drill, and his innovations\ncreated the modern oil drilling industry.\n在遭遇几次挫折后，卢卡斯上尉决定改用钻机，他的革新开创了现代石油钻探业。\n【信号】信号词 created 点出方法的成果。his innovations created the modern oil drilling\nindustry 里 created（开创）直接交代这套方法的历史成果，问 say about method 时，\n听到这种交代成果、影响的动词就锁定该句。\n【替换】his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry ↔\nIt gave birth to the oil drilling industry.\ncreated 同义替换为 gave birth to\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 说该方法 rendered many oil workers jobless（使许多石油工人失业），录音只说它开\n创了钻探业，从没提失业，凭对新技术抢饭碗的常识联想出来，原文无据。\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说 It was not as effective as he claimed.（不如他宣称的有效），录音对该方法是正面\n肯定 his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry，B 把肯定改成否定质\n疑，极性相反。\n借用原文：his innovations created the modern oil drilling industry\nD) 未提及\nD 说 It was not popularized until years later.（多年后才普及），录音只讲该法当即开创\n了行业，没说推广被推迟，时间信息属虚构。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What do we learn about Texas oil industry boom?", "options": ["A) It radically transformed the state's economy.", "B) It resulted in an oil surplus all over the world.", "C) It totally destroyed the state's rural landscape.", "D) It ruined the state's cotton and beef industries."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It radically transformed the state's economy.\n【定位】In those days, Texas was almost entirely rural, with no large cities and practically no industry;\ncotton and beef were the foundation of the economy. Higgins' well changed that.\n那时候，德州几乎全是乡村，没有大城市、几乎没有工业，棉花和牛肉是经济的支柱。希金\n斯的油井改变了这一切。\n【信号】转折信号 changed that 是结论落点。录音先铺垫德州 almost entirely rural 的旧貌，紧\n接一句 Higgins' well changed that，changed that（改变了这一切）是从旧貌转向新局\n的转折落点，答案就在这一转之后，听到 changed that 即锁定。\n【替换】Texas was almost entirely rural, with no large cities and practically no industry; cotton and beef\nwere the foundation of the economy. Higgins' well changed that\n↔ It radically transformed the state's economy.\n从农业旧貌到 changed that 概括为彻底改变经济\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB 说造成 oil surplus all over the world（全世界石油过剩），录音只说 the sudden\nsurplus of petroleum was not entirely a blessing for Texas，过剩是德州本地的事，B 盗\n用 surplus 一词却把范围放大到全世界，范围被夸大。\n借用原文：the sudden surplus of petroleum was not entirely a blessing for Texas\nC) 过度推断\nC 说 totally destroyed the state's rural landscape（彻底毁掉乡村景观），录音确实说德\n州曾 almost entirely rural，但只讲经济被改变，从没说乡村景观被毁，盗用 rural 一词加\ntotally 夸大。\n借用原文：Texas was almost entirely rural\nD) 偷换概念\nD 说 ruined the state's cotton and beef industries（毁掉棉花和牛肉业），录音里\ncotton and beef were the foundation of the economy 是讲它们曾是旧支柱、后被石油\n经济取代，并未说被毁掉，盗用 cotton and beef 偷换成 ruined。\n借用原文：cotton and beef were the foundation of the economy", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What is a primary cause of employee dissatisfaction according to recent research?", "options": ["A) Unsuitable jobs.", "B) Bad managers.", "C) Insufficient motivation.", "D) Tough regulations."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Bad managers.\n【定位】A primary cause of employee dissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that many believe\nthey have terrible managers.\n据最新研究，员工不满的一个主要原因是，许多人认为自己摊上了糟糕的管理者。\n【信号】题干原话几乎照搬的点题句。录音开篇第二句就用 A primary cause of employee\ndissatisfaction, according to fresh research, is that…，与题干 a primary cause of\nemployee dissatisfaction according to recent research 几乎逐词对应——这种「题干就\n是录音句的复述」是最强的答案句信号，is that 之后的内容即答案。\n【替换】they have terrible managers ↔ Bad managers.\nterrible 同义改写为 bad\n【排除】A) 未提及\nUnsuitable jobs.（不合适的岗位）录音通篇未提，整段都在讲管理者的问题，从没说员工\n不满是因为岗位不对口，属凭常识自行脑补的归因。\nC) 原词陷阱\nInsufficient motivation.（动力不足）看似呼应录音后文谈管理者「缺乏 drive（干劲）」\n的描述，但那是在说坏管理者的个人特征（第 4 段），不是员工不满的主因；它把「动力」\n这个似曾相识的概念安到了错误的问题上。\n借用原文：those without drive\nD) 答非所问\nTough regulations.（严苛的规章）勉强能让人联想到第 3 段的 toxic 工作环境，但录音从\n未把「规章严苛」列为员工不满的主因；本题问的是开篇点明的 primary cause，规章既非\n原话、也非主因。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What is one of the causes for poor management practices?", "options": ["A) Ineffective training.", "B) Toxic company culture.", "C) Lack of regular evaluation.", "D) Overburdening of managers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Toxic company culture.\n【定位】The first cause was company culture, which was seen by employees as enabling poor\nmanagement practices. It was specifically stressful work environments, minimal training and\na lack of accountability that were found to be the most blameworthy. Such workplaces are\nsometimes described as toxic.\n第一个成因是公司文化，员工认为它纵容了差劲的管理做法。具体而言，最该受责备的是充\n满压力的工作环境、极少的培训和缺乏问责制。这样的职场有时被形容为「有毒的」。\n【信号】分点信号 The first cause + 收尾点出 toxic。录音用 The first cause was company\nculture 起头明确这是分点罗列的第一条成因，又在段末用 Such workplaces are\nsometimes described as toxic. 把这种公司文化点名为 toxic（有毒的）——首句立题、末\n句给出关键词，前后夹住的就是答案句。\n【替换】The first cause was company culture ↔ Toxic company culture.\ncompany culture 原词 + toxic 由原文回收\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIneffective training.（无效的培训）盗用了录音 minimal training（极少的培训）里的\ntraining 一词来勾人，但录音说的是培训「太少」，不是培训「无效」；况且 minimal\ntraining 只是 company culture 这条大成因下的一个细节，本身不是独立成因。\n借用原文：minimal training\nC) 偷换概念\nLack of regular evaluation.（缺少定期评估）借用了录音 a lack of accountability（缺乏\n问责）的 lack of 句式来制造耳熟感，但 accountability（问责）≠ evaluation（评估），\n录音从未说缺少「定期评估」；这是把一个近形概念偷换进来。\n借用原文：a lack of accountability\nD) 未提及\nOverburdening of managers.（管理者负担过重）听起来像在替管理者开脱，但录音四条\n成因（公司文化、管理者性格、资历不足、靠资历晋升）里没有一条说管理者「负担过\n重」，纯属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What do we learn about the study on job dissatisfaction?", "options": ["A) It collected feedback from both employers and employees.", "B) It was conducted from frontline managers' point of view.", "C) It provided meaningful clues to solving the problem.", "D) It was based only on the perspective of employees."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It was based only on the perspective of employees.\n【定位】A point worth making is that the study was based only on the perspective of employees. The\nresearchers didn't ask senior leaders what they thought of their frontline managers.\n有一点值得说明：这项研究仅仅基于员工的视角。研究者并没有去问高层领导他们如何看待\n自己手下的一线管理者。\n【信号】结尾提示语 A point worth making + only。录音末段用 A point worth making is\nthat…（有一点值得说明）这种「特意提醒」的强调语引出关键句，后面紧跟 the study\nwas based only on the perspective of employees，only 一词锁死研究的取材范围——\n结尾的特意强调语就是答案句信号。\n【替换】the study was based only on the perspective of employees ↔\nIt was based only on the perspective of employees.\n几乎逐词对应的复述\nThe researchers didn't ask senior leaders ↔ only on the perspective of employees\n否定句从反面印证 only\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nIt collected feedback from both employers and employees.（向雇主和雇员双方收集反\n馈）盗用了录音里真实出现的 employees 一词，却把范围从「只问员工」夸成「双方都\n问」；录音明说 The researchers didn't ask senior leaders，恰恰否定了 both。\n借用原文：the study was based only on the perspective of employees\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt was conducted from frontline managers' point of view.（研究从一线管理者的视角进\n行）搬来了录音 their frontline managers 这个原词，但录音说研究是从员工视角做的，\nfrontline managers 是被评价的对象、不是研究的视角；选项把视角张冠李戴了。\n借用原文：what they thought of their frontline managers\nC) 过度推断\nIt provided meaningful clues to solving the problem.（研究为解决问题提供了有价值的\n线索）是一句听起来很顺、很积极的评价，但末段只交代研究的取材局限（只问员工、高层\n没问），从没说它给出了「解决问题的有价值线索」，属凭常识脑补的拔高。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the passage say about the mining industry in Western Australia?", "options": ["A) It is seeing an automation revolution.", "B) It is bringing prosperity to the region.", "C) It is yielding an unprecedented profit.", "D) It is expanding at an accelerating speed."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is seeing an automation revolution.\n【定位】With the use of driverless vehicles seemingly inevitable, mining companies in the vast\nAustralian desert state of Western Australia are definitely taking the lead.\n在无人驾驶车辆的使用看似不可避免的背景下，澳大利亚西部这个辽阔沙漠州的矿业公司无\n疑正走在前列。\n【信号】开头点题的总括句。篇章独白的总判断常落在开头：录音第一句 mining companies in the\nvast Australian desert state of Western Australia are definitely taking the lead 直接给\n出西澳矿业的整体定位——在自动化（无人驾驶车辆）上领先，这就是答案句。\n【替换】driverless vehicles ↔ automation\n无人驾驶=自动化的同义改写\ntaking the lead ↔ revolution\n领先变革=同主题呼应\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 bringing prosperity to the region 借用了录音里反复出现的地域词（desert state、\nremote communities）制造熟悉感，但录音第一句讲的是该矿业领先于自动化，并未说它\n给地区带来繁荣，属听到地域词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：the area's remoteness\nC) 未提及\n选项 yielding an unprecedented profit 听上去与矿业赚钱顺理成章，但录音开头讲的是技\n术领先，全文只提到 financial imperatives、more productive 这类追求效率的动因，从未\n说产出空前利润，属未提及。\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 expanding at an accelerating speed 蹭了录音里 taking the lead、a technological\nleap 的进取气势，但录音说的是矿业在自动化技术上领先、是技术跃进，而非整个行业在加\n速扩张规模，把技术领先偷换成了规模扩张。\n借用原文：They are a technological leap", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is the impact of the digitization of the mining industry?", "options": ["A) It exhausts resources sooner.", "B) It creates a lot of new jobs.", "C) It causes conflicts between employers and employees.", "D) It calls for the retraining of unskilled mining workers."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It creates a lot of new jobs.\n【定位】The automation and digitization of the industry are creating a need for different jobs. These\ninclude data scientists and engineers in automation and artificial intelligence.\n这个行业的自动化与数字化正在催生对不同岗位的需求。这些岗位包括数据科学家以及自动\n化和人工智能领域的工程师。\n【信号】因果动词 creating 引出影响。录音第 6 段 The automation and digitization of the\nindustry are creating a need for different jobs 用 creating a need for 直接点出数字化\n的后果是催生新岗位，正对题干所问的 impact，是答案句。\n【替换】creating a need for different jobs ↔ creates a lot of new jobs\n催生岗位需求=创造新岗位\ndata scientists and engineers in automation and artificial intelligence ↔ a lot of new jobs\n举例的具体岗位=新岗位的实证\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 exhausts resources sooner 借矿业必然涉及资源的常识制造合理感，但录音通篇讲数\n字化带来岗位变化与可持续，从未说它更快耗尽资源，原文无此判断。\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 conflicts between employers and employees 蹭了录音里 employees、workers'\nunions 这些词，但录音讲工会有保留是出于对偏远社区的担忧，并未说数字化在劳资双方\n间引发冲突，把工会的保留偷换成了劳资对立。\n借用原文：Workers' unions have accepted the inevitability of the introduction of new technology\nD) 偷换概念\n选项 retraining of unskilled mining workers 借用了录音里的 mining、workers，但录音\n说数字化需要的是数据科学家、工程师这类新型人才，并未提对非技术矿工的再培训，把催\n生新岗位偷换成了再培训旧工人。\n借用原文：data scientists and engineers in automation and artificial intelligence", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What is the attitude of workers' unions towards the introduction of new technology?", "options": ["A) They welcome it with open arms.", "B) They will wait to see its effect.", "C) They are strongly opposed to it.", "D) They accept it with reservations."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） They accept it with reservations.\n【定位】Workers' unions have accepted the inevitability of the introduction of new technology. But\nthey still have reservations about the rise of automation technology.\n工会已经接受了引入新技术不可避免这一现实。但他们对自动化技术的兴起仍有保留意见。\n【信号】转折 But 后才是真态度。录音先说工会 accepted（接受），紧接 But they still have\nreservations 用 But 转出真态度——仍有保留。态度题的落点在转折之后，前半的接受只\n是铺垫。\n【替换】have accepted the inevitability ↔ accept it\n接受=accept，态度同向\nstill have reservations ↔ with reservations\n仍有保留=with reservations\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 welcome it with open arms 把工会的态度拔高成热烈欢迎，只接住了 But 前的\naccepted，丢了 But 后的 reservations，态度偏正、过头了。\n借用原文：have accepted the inevitability of the introduction of new technology\nB) 原词陷阱\n选项 wait to see its effect 借用了录音里讲影响（impact、effect）的语境制造熟悉感，但\n录音说工会已经接受、只是有保留，并非按兵不动地观望效果，原文无观望此意。\n借用原文：the most significant impact of robotic technology\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 strongly opposed to it 抓住录音里 reservations、main concern 的负面情绪，把有\n保留放大成强烈反对，但工会明说已经 accepted，并未反对，态度极性反了。\n借用原文：they still have reservations about the rise of automation technology", "points": ["听力·讲座", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say about traffic accidents in Thailand?", "options": ["A) Their cost to the nation's economy is incalculable.", "B) They kill more people than any infectious disease.", "C) Their annual death rate is about twice that of the global average.", "D) They have experienced a gradual decline since the year of 2017."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Their annual death rate is about twice that of the global average.\n【定位】According to official statistics, Thailand's annual road-death rate is almost double the\nglobal average.\n据官方统计，泰国的年度道路死亡率几乎是全球平均水平的两倍。\n【信号】地名加数字即落点。第一段开门见山点出 Thailand's annual road-death rate is almost\ndouble the global average，地名 Thailand 加上倍数 double 同时出现，正是预读时盯的\n两个锚点，这一句就是答案句。\n【替换】almost double the global average ↔ about twice that of the global average\ndouble 同义于 twice\nThailand's annual road-death rate ↔ Their annual death rate\n代词回指加省略\n【排除】A) 未提及\nTheir cost to the nation's economy is incalculable. 谈经济成本不可估量，录音第一段只\n说死亡率高、并说这本可改变，从未提及对经济的成本，属凭常识脑补的无中生有。\nB) 张冠李戴\nThey kill more people than any infectious disease. 里的 infectious disease 确实在录音\n出现，但那是第二段讲全球情况 road accidents kill more people every year than any\ninfectious disease，主语是 Globally 而非泰国，被错安到了泰国头上。\n借用原文：road accidents kill more people every year than any infectious disease\nD) 与原文相反\nThey have experienced a gradual decline since the year of 2017 说泰国死亡率自 2017\n年逐渐下降，2017 这个年份录音第二段 the death toll in 2017 确实念到，但那是全球死\n亡人数的统计年份，且原文说泰国道路危险本可改变、并无下降之说，极性正好相反。\n借用原文：the death toll in 2017", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do we learn from an American institute's statistics regarding road deaths?", "options": ["A) They show a difference between rich and poor nations.", "B) They don't reflect the changes in individual countries.", "C) They rise and fall from year to year.", "D) They are not as reliable as claimed."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They don't reflect the changes in individual countries.\n【定位】According to the institute, the overall number of deaths has been more or less static since the\nturn of the century. But that disguises a lot of changes in individual countries.\n据该机构称，自世纪之交以来，死亡总数大体保持平稳。但这掩盖了各个国家内部的大量\n变化。\n【信号】转折 But 后才是真意。前半句说总数基本平稳，紧接 But that disguises a lot of changes\nin individual countries，转折词 But 之后才是说话人真正强调的那层意思——这组数据掩\n盖了个体国家的变化，正是答案落点。\n【替换】that disguises a lot of changes in individual countries ↔\nThey don't reflect the changes in individual countries.\ndisguise 转写为 don't reflect\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThey show a difference between rich and poor nations. 说贫富差异——贫富国家的对\n比确在录音第三段出现 In many poor countries … In many rich countries, by\ncontrast，但那是后文展开的内容，并非这组美国机构统计本身得出的结论，题干问的恰是\n这组统计，张冠李戴了。\n借用原文：In many poor countries … In many rich countries, by contrast\nC) 与原文相反\nThey rise and fall from year to year. 说死亡数逐年起落，恰与原文 has been more or\nless static since the turn of the century（大体保持平稳）相反，把平稳说成了波动。\n借用原文：has been more or less static since the turn of the century\nD) 未提及\nThey are not as reliable as claimed. 质疑这组美国机构统计的可靠性，但录音对这组数据\n只说它平稳、它掩盖了各国变化，从未说它不可靠（说统计不充分的是第三段的穷国官方统\n计，非这组）。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What is said about middle-income countries?", "options": ["A) Many of them have increasing numbers of cars on the road.", "B) Many of them are following the example set by Thailand.", "C) Many of them have seen a decline in road-death rates.", "D) Many of them are investing heavily in infrastructure."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Many of them have seen a decline in road-death rates.\n【定位】But the most important and intriguing changes are taking place in middle-income countries…\nAccording to researchers, in China and South Africa, traffic deaths have been falling since\n2000, and in India since 2012, and the Philippines reached its peak four years ago.\n但最重要也最耐人寻味的变化正发生在中等收入国家……据研究者称，在中国和南非，交通\n死亡数自 2000 年起持续下降，印度自 2012 年起下降，菲律宾在四年前达到峰值。\n【信号】群体名加并列实例。第四段点出 middle-income countries 后，立即用 China、South\nAfrica、India、Philippines 一连串实例说明 traffic deaths have been falling，群体名加\n并列例证处即答案句。\n【替换】traffic deaths have been falling since 2000 ↔\nMany of them have seen a decline in road-death rates.\nfalling 名词化为 a decline\nin China and South Africa … in India … the Philippines ↔ Many of them\n多国列举概括为 Many of them\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nMany of them have increasing numbers of cars on the road. 说车辆增多，increasing\n与车确在录音出现，但那是第三段讲穷国 a fast-growing fleet of cars and motorbikes，\n说的是低收入国家而非中等收入国家，主体错位。\n借用原文：a fast-growing fleet of cars and motorbikes\nB) 与原文相反\nMany of them are following the example set by Thailand. 说这些国家效仿泰国，可原\n文恰恰相反——whether Thailand can soon follow suit，是问泰国能否效仿这些国家，方\n向被颠倒了。\n借用原文：whether Thailand can soon follow suit\nD) 原词陷阱\nMany of them are investing heavily in infrastructure. 里的 infrastructure 确在录音末段\n出现 not just in better infrastructure，但那是讲解决办法、且说光靠基建不够，根本没说\n中等收入国家在大举投资基建，属原词陷阱式的答非所问。\n借用原文：not just in better infrastructure", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What else could be done to reduce fatal road accidents in addition to safer roads?", "options": ["A) Foster better driving behavior.", "B) Provide better training for drivers.", "C) Abolish all outdated traffic rules.", "D) Impose heavier penalties on speeding."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Foster better driving behavior.\n【定位】The solution lies not just in better infrastructure, but in better social incentives. Safe driving\nhabits are practices which people know they should follow but often don't. People respond to\nincentives, such as traffic laws that are actually enforced.\n解决之道不只在于更好的基础设施，更在于更好的社会激励。安全驾驶是人们明知该做却常\n常不做的事。人们会对激励作出反应，比如真正得到执行的交通法规。\n【信号】提对策句 solution lies in。末段直接给方案 The solution lies not just in better\ninfrastructure, but in better social incentives，The solution lies in 是典型的提对策信\n号，紧接着 Safe driving habits 把社会激励落到培养安全驾驶习惯，这一串就是答案句。\n【替换】better social incentives ↔ Foster better driving behavior.\n概念落地的同义改写\n【排除】B) 未提及\nProvide better training for drivers. 说给司机更好的培训，听上去很对症，但录音只说要\n靠社会激励养成习惯，从未提到培训 driver，属凭常识脑补。\nC) 与原文相反\nAbolish all outdated traffic rules. 里的 traffic 与法规确呼应录音 traffic laws that are\nactually enforced，但原文是要让交通法规真正得到执行，选项却说废除过时规则，方向相\n反，且 all、outdated 更是无中生有的绝对化。\n借用原文：traffic laws that are actually enforced\nD) 过度推断\nImpose heavier penalties on speeding. 顺着 traffic laws that are actually enforced 的\n执法语境联想出加重处罚、罚超速，可原文只说让法规真正被执行，既没说加重\npenalties，也没专门提 speeding，属过度推断。\n借用原文：traffic laws that are actually enforced", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) investigated\n动词\n【词性槽】主语后缺谓语动词\n【依据】「研究 / 调查」最贴学者动作\n宾语是 the cultural diversity of children's books（童书的文化多样性），又呼应下一句\nShe examined the books __(27)__ in the kindergarten rooms of four day-care centers\nin Western Australia. 中的 examined（考察），investigated 与 examined 几乎同义，\n构成「研究—考察」的同义复现链，最契合学者做研究的语境。\n【竞争词】J) safeguarded 语义反向：保护 ≠ 研究\nsafeguarded 意为「捍卫 / 保护」，搭配宾语应是要保护的对象（如权利、安全）。研究者的动\n作是「考察」童书的多样性，而非「保护」多样性，且与下文 examined 完全不构成同义复现，\n语义指向错位。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语后缺谓语动词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) housed\n动词\n【词性槽】名词后非谓语，作后置定语\n【依据】books housed in… = 存放于…的书\nhouse 作动词意为「容纳 / 存放」，过去分词 housed 修饰 books，意为「存放在日托中\n心教室里的那些书」，正好对应研究者「考察了四家日托中心里现存的童书」的研究设计。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词后非谓语，作后置定语"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) representation\n名词\n【词性槽】any + 形容词 + ___ + of\n【依据】「对…的表现 / 描绘」是固定搭配\nrepresentation of sb. 意为「对某人的描绘 / 形象呈现」，是讨论文学 / 媒体中群体形象的\n高频术语；与首句 cultural inclusiveness reflected in the children's literature 中的「文\n化包容性的体现」同根呼应。只有 18% 的书里出现非白人「形象」，完美匹配主旨。\n【竞争词】B) appreciation appreciation of 是「欣赏」不是「描绘」\nappreciation of non-white people 会被理解为「对非白人的欣赏 / 感激」，与「书里包含 X」\n的语义不通——书里包含的不是「欣赏」这种心理活动，而是「形象 / 出现」。\nD) fraud 语义完全偏题\nfraud 是「欺诈 / 骗子」，与儿童读物多样性主题毫无关联，属一眼排除。\nL) superiority 搭配反向\nsuperiority of non-white people 意为「非白人的优越性」，与全文「白人占优势、少数族裔\n被边缘」的论述方向完全相反。\nM) temperament 「性情」与「书的内容」不沾\ntemperament 指人的气质 / 性情，搭配应是 a calm temperament 之类，不能用「书里包含某\n人的 temperament」来谈书籍多样性。\nO) threshold 「门槛 / 临界值」语义不沾\nthreshold of 多指 pain threshold（疼痛阈值）或 the threshold of a new era（新时代门\n槛），与「书中是否出现某群体」无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·any + 形容词 + ___ + of"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) portraying\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 by 后必接动名词\n【依据】「用筷子和传统服饰刻画亚洲文化」\nportray sth. with sth. = 用某物来描绘 / 刻画某物，是固定搭配。整句要表达「刻板地呈现\n亚洲文化的方式之一就是只用筷子和传统服饰来刻画」，与前句 stereotypical or\ntokenistic ways 的「刻板化呈现」一脉相承。\n【竞争词】A) alienating 「疏远」与例子动作不符\nalienating Asian culture with chopsticks 意为「用筷子使亚洲文化被疏远」，逻辑别扭——筷\n子是「描绘亚洲文化的刻板符号」，不是「使其疏远的工具」。而且 alienating 一词在最后一段\n还有专门的空格（34 题）要用，提前用掉会破坏全卷词义分布。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 by 后必接动名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) secondary\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词前作定语\n【依据】与下句「主角多为白人」形成主次对照\n紧接下文 The main characters were mostly Caucasian.，主角 main characters 是白\n人，那少数族裔角色自然就是「次要 / 配角」。secondary roles 正好与 main characters\n构成 main vs secondary 的二元对照，是命题人埋的同义反义复现的标志性线索。\n【竞争词】G) overwhelming 程度与逻辑都反\noverwhelming roles 意为「压倒性 / 主导的角色」，与后一句 main characters were mostly\nCaucasian（主角多是白人）矛盾——若少数族裔角色压倒性主导，作者就不会得出「白人更有\n价值」的担忧。\nN) tentative 搭配不成立\ntentative 常修饰 plan / agreement / smile（试探性的计划 / 协议 / 微笑），不修饰 roles。即\n便强译为「试探性的角色」也不符英语习惯，且与「主次对照」的语境无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词前作定语"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) bias\n名词\n【词性槽】own-race + ___ + from…of age\n【依据】own-race bias 是心理学固定术语\nown-race bias（同族偏好 / 本族偏见）是发展心理学高频术语，指婴幼儿对同族面孔\n的偏好。本句说「孩子三个月大就开始发展 own-race ___」，与该术语完全吻合；又呼\n应上句 children formed impressions about \"difference\" and identity from a very\nyoung age.。\n【竞争词】D) fraud 语义不沾\nfraud 是「欺诈」，与婴儿发展心理学的语境毫无关联。\nM) temperament 搭配不成立\nown-race temperament 不是英语固定搭配；temperament 与「种族」不直接组合，且与\nfrom three months of age develop 的「逐渐发展出来」语义弱相关——气质多被视作天生而\n非「发展出」的。\nO) threshold 「门槛」与「同族倾向」不沾\nown-race threshold 没有这种说法；threshold 常与 pain / sensory 搭配，与种族认知偏好\n无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·own-race + ___ + fro"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) appreciation\n名词\n【词性槽】三个 of diversity 并列名词的第三个\n【依据】「理解—接受—欣赏」三步递进\nappreciation of diversity（对多样性的欣赏）是讨论多元文化教育的固定表达，与前两项\nunderstanding, acceptance 构成「理解→接受→欣赏」的程度递进，是英语写作的经典三\n段式。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·三个 of diversity 并列名词"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) overwhelming\n形容词\n【词性槽】the + ___ + 名词 promotion\n【依据】「压倒性的推崇」呼应主旨张力\n全文要说的正是「白人中产观念在童书中占据压倒性比例」（开头 18% 的数据即为佐\n证）。overwhelming promotion = 压倒性的推崇 / 推广，与下句 risks alienating\nchildren from minority groups（有疏远少数族裔孩子的风险）形成「压倒性优势 → 边缘\n化少数」的因果链，逻辑自洽。\n【竞争词】N) tentative 「试探性的」与下文「风险」矛盾\ntentative promotion 意为「试探性的、不肯定的推广」，若白人观念只是试探性推广，就不至\n于到「疏远少数族裔」的程度；与下文 risks alienating 的严重后果在程度上完全不匹配，且与\n开篇 bleak picture（黯淡景象）的语调相悖。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— tentative 看似中性最易误选。形容词槽 the current ___\npromotion 里，tentative（试探性的）听上去「学术腔」很容易被选。防坑要点：往下读\n到 risks alienating（有疏远的风险）这种强后果词，立刻意识到前面要选「程度强」的\noverwhelming，而不是「程度弱」的 tentative。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the + ___ + 名词 promo"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) alienating\n动词\n【词性槽】risk + V-ing\n【依据】「使少数族裔孩子被疏远」\nalienate sb. 意为「使某人疏远 / 被排斥在外」。整句意为「这种压倒性推崇白人中产观念\n的做法，有让少数族裔孩子被边缘化的风险」，与上一段 Books can also allow children\nto see diversity. 的反面后果对应，逻辑闭合。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·risk + V-ing"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alienating", "B) appreciation", "C) bias", "D) fraud", "E) housed", "F) investigated", "G) overwhelming", "H) portraying", "I) representation", "J) safeguarded", "K) secondary", "L) superiority", "M) temperament", "N) tentative", "O) threshold"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) superiority\n名词\n【词性槽】a sense of + ___ + or privilege\n【依据】「优越感」与「特权」天然并列\nsuperiority（优越感）和 privilege（特权）是讨论种族 / 阶级议题的经典并列词组；与开\n篇 This causes concern as it can lead to an impression that whiteness is of greater\nvalue. 直接呼应——「白人更有价值」的印象一旦内化，就会成为白人中产儿童的「优越感\n或特权感」。\n【竞争词】B) appreciation 感情色彩反向\nappreciation 是「欣赏 / 感激」，带褒义；而本句要表达的是「这种推崇带来的负面副作用」，\n与 privilege 的批评性语境不符。\nD) fraud 「欺诈」与「特权」不在同一范畴\na sense of fraud 通常指「自己是骗子的感觉」，与白人中产儿童「享受白人主流呈现」的语境\n完全不搭。\nM) temperament 「性情」属于个体气质\ntemperament 指与生俱来的性情，不可与社会建构的 privilege 并列；且 a sense of\ntemperament 不是地道搭配。\nO) threshold 「门槛」与「特权」语义错位\nthreshold 指起点 / 临界值，常说 a sense of achievement / accomplishment 才贴近，a\nsense of threshold 不成立。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— appreciation 看似与「特权」并列也通。a sense of\nappreciation 是地道搭配（感激之情），考生容易顺手选 appreciation。防坑要点：or\nprivilege 的并列把语义范畴锁死在「上位者的优势感」，appreciation 是褒义的「欣赏 /\n感激」，与 privilege 不同范畴；而且 appreciation 早在 32 题就已被「understanding,\nacceptance and ___」的递进结构锁定，先做 32 题就能自然把它从 35 题划走。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a sense of + ___ + o"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Marconi was central to our present-day understanding of communication.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对现代通信的中心理解\n【定位】Marconi was quite simply the central figure in the emergence of a modern understanding\nof communication.\n马可尼简直就是现代通信理解兴起过程中的中心人物。\n【改写】central to our present-day understanding of communication ↔\nthe central figure in the emergence of a modern understanding of communication\ncentral 一对一、present-day 等于 modern\n【辨邻】 A A 段虽然也是总评段，但说的是 laid the foundation of the communication\nexplosion 与 the first truly global figure，强调的是奠基者与第一这一身份，并未提\n到 central to our present-day understanding 中的 central 与 understanding 这一\n组合。\nlaid the foundation of the communication explosion of the 21st century", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "As an adult, Marconi had an intuition that he had to be loyal to politicians in order to be influential.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】成年时直觉效忠政治家\n【定位】by the time he reached adulthood, he understood, intuitively, that in order to have an impact\nhe had to both develop an independent economic base and align himself with political power.\n到成年时，他凭直觉就明白：要产生影响，他必须既建立独立的经济基础，又让自己与政治\n权力结盟。\n【改写】As an adult ↔ by the time he reached adulthood\n成年的两种基础表达\nhad an intuition ↔ understood, intuitively\n名词与副词的同根转换\nhad to be loyal to politicians in order to be influential ↔\nin order to have an impact he had to both develop an independent economic base and align\nhimself with political power\n结盟即忠诚、有影响即有效力\n【辨邻】 K K 段虽然也讲忠诚于政治（served Mussolini faithfully），但陈述句 had an\nintuition 强调的是认知形成阶段（成年时的直觉判断），K 段讲的是已成定局的具体\n效忠行为，时间维度与认知维度都不对应。\nafter a lifetime of moving within the circles of power, he was unable to break with authority", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Marconi disapproved of the use of wireless communication for commercial broadcasting.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】反对将无线用于商业广播\n【定位】There are some who say Marconi lost his edge when commercial broadcasting came along; he\ndidn't see that radio could or should be used to frivolous(无聊的) ends.\n有人说当商业广播兴起时，马可尼已经落伍；他不认为广播可以或应该被用作无聊的目的。\n【改写】disapproved of the use of wireless communication for commercial broadcasting ↔\nhe didn't see that radio could or should be used to frivolous(无聊的) ends\n含蓄否定表达不赞成 + radio 即 wireless", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Marconi's example demonstrates that theoretical concepts and experiments complement each other in making progress in science and technology.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】理论与实验互补\n【定位】Marconi proved that theory and experimentation were complementary features of progress.\n马可尼证明了理论与实验是进步的互补特征。\n【改写】theoretical concepts and experiments complement each other ↔\ntheory and experimentation were complementary features of progress\ncomplement 同根 + theory/experimentation 同根\nmaking progress in science and technology ↔\nbring about a revolution in the technique of radio-communications\n进步与革命的同义概括", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Marconi's real interest lay in the development of worldwide wireless communication.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】真正兴趣在全球无线通信\n【定位】Marconi was really interested in only one thing: the extension of mobile, personal, long-\ndistance communication to the ends of the earth (and beyond, if we can believe some reports).\n马可尼真正关心的只有一件事：把移动、个人化、远距离通信延伸到地球的尽头（如果有报\n道可信的话，甚至更远）。\n【改写】Marconi's real interest ↔ Marconi was really interested in only one thing\nreal/really 同根 + only one thing 强化\nthe development of worldwide wireless communication ↔\nthe extension of mobile, personal, long-distance communication to the ends of the earth\nextension 即 development、to the ends of the earth 即 worldwide\n【辨邻】 E E 段说 Marconi's career was devoted to making wireless communication\nhappen cheaply, efficiently, smoothly，描述的是他职业生涯做无线通信的方式与品\n质标准，并未用 real interest 这种动机性表述去定位他唯一关心的事。\nMarconi's career was devoted to making wireless communication happen cheaply,\nefficiently, smoothly", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Marconi spent his whole life making wireless communication simple to use.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】毕生让无线通信易用\n【定位】Marconi's career was devoted to making wireless communication happen cheaply, efficiently,\nsmoothly, and with an elegance that would appear to be intuitive and uncomplicated to the\nuser-friendly, if you will.\n马可尼的职业生涯致力于让无线通信变得便宜、高效、流畅，且优雅得在用户看来直觉而不\n复杂——也就是用户友好。\n【改写】spent his whole life ↔ Marconi's career was devoted to\ncareer devoted 即 spent whole life\nmaking wireless communication simple to use ↔\nmaking wireless communication happen cheaply, efficiently, smoothly, and with an elegance\nthat would appear to be intuitive and uncomplicated to the user-friendly\nuser-friendly 与 simple to use 完全同义\n【辨邻】 C C 段 Marconi was really interested in only one thing: the extension of mobile,\npersonal, long-distance communication to the ends of the earth 讲的是把通信延\n伸到多远，是范围维度；陈述句 simple to use 强调的是让用户用起来简单，是用户体\n验维度。\nthe extension of mobile, personal, long-distance communication to the ends of the earth", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Because of his long-time connection with people in power, Marconi was unable to cut himself off from the fascist regime in Italy.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无法与意大利法西斯政权切割\n【定位】However, after a lifetime of moving within the circles of power, he was unable to break with\nauthority, and served Mussolini faithfully (as president of Italy's national research council and\nroyal academy, as well as a member of the Fascist Grand Council) until the day he died—\nconveniently—in 1937, shortly before he would have had to take a stand in the conflict that\nconsumed a world that he had, in part, created.\n然而，经过一生在权力圈中游走，他已无法与权威决裂，忠诚服侍墨索里尼（任意大利国家\n研究委员会与皇家学院主席、法西斯大议会成员），直到 1937 年他便利地去世——就在他\n本会被迫表态于那场吞噬世界的冲突之前不久，而这世界一定程度上正是由他参与创造的。\n【改写】his long-time connection with people in power ↔\na lifetime of moving within the circles of power\nlong-time 等于 lifetime、people in power 等于 circles of power\nunable to cut himself off from the fascist regime in Italy ↔\nhe was unable to break with authority, and served Mussolini faithfully\ncut off 等于 break with、fascist regime 等于 Mussolini\n【辨邻】 I I 段 align himself with political power 描述的是马可尼成年时的认知决定（决定要靠\n拢政治），属于动机层；陈述句 unable to cut himself off 是后期已成定局的无能\n层，是 K 段才有的内容。\nalign himself with political power", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "In his later years, Marconi exerted a tremendous influence on all aspects of people's life.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】晚年对生活各方面巨大影响\n【定位】By then, anything Marconi said or did was newsworthy. Stock prices rose or sank according\nto his pronouncements. If Marconi said he thought it might rain, there was likely to be a run\non umbrellas.\n到那时，马可尼说什么做什么都是新闻；股价随他的言论涨跌；他若说可能下雨，就会有抢\n购雨伞的风潮。\n【改写】In his later years ↔ Two months before he died, newspapers were reporting\n去世前两月即晚年\nexerted a tremendous influence on all aspects of people's life ↔\nStock prices rose or sank according to his pronouncements. If Marconi said he thought it\nmight rain, there was likely to be a run on umbrellas.\n抽象概括由两个具体例证承载\n【辨邻】 A A 段 A hundred years before iconic figures like Bill Gates and Steve Jobs\npermeated our lives 讲的是百年尺度的奠基影响，不是 later years 这一晚年具体时\n段的影响。\nA hundred years before iconic figures like Bill Gates and Steve Jobs permeated our lives", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "What connected the 19th century and our present time was the development of wireless communication.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无线通信连接 19 世纪与现在\n【定位】What made the link from then to now was the development of wireless communication.\n把那时和现在连接起来的，正是无线通信的发展。\n【改写】What connected the 19th century and our present time ↔ What made the link from then to now\nconnected 等于 made the link、present time 等于 now\nthe development of wireless communication ↔ the development of wireless communication\n原文整短语逐字复现", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Despite his autonomy, Marconi felt alienated and suffered from a lack of acceptance.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】自主却疏离、苦于不被接纳\n【定位】At one level, Marconi could be fiercely autonomous and independent of the constraints of his\nown social class. On another scale, he was a perpetual outsider. Wherever he went, he was\nnever \"of\" the group; he was always the \"other,\" considered foreign in Britain, British in Italy,\nand \"not American\" in the United States. At the same time, he also suffered tremendously\nfrom a need for acceptance that drove, and sometimes stained, every one of his relationships.\n一方面，马可尼可以激烈地自主，独立于他自身阶级的束缚；另一方面，他又是永远的外\n人。无论走到哪儿，他都不属于那个群体；永远是那个他者——在英国被视为外国人、在意\n大利被视为英国人、在美国被视为非美国人。与此同时，他也极度苦于对被接纳的渴求，这\n种渴求驱动并有时玷污了他的每一段关系。\n【改写】Despite his autonomy ↔\nMarconi could be fiercely autonomous and independent of the constraints of his own social\nclass\nautonomy 与 autonomous 同根\nfelt alienated ↔\nhe was a perpetual outsider … he was never \"of\" the group; he was always the \"other,\"\noutsider/other 即 alienated\nsuffered from a lack of acceptance ↔ he also suffered tremendously from a need for acceptance\n渴求接纳即缺乏接纳的正话反说\n【辨邻】 K K 段 he began to suffer a crisis of conscience 讲的是政治选择上的良心冲突，不是\nalienated 这种社会身份疏离感，也不涉及 lack of acceptance 这种渴求接纳。\nhe began to suffer a crisis of conscience", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What did a recent research paper claim?", "options": ["A) The type of school students attend makes a difference to their future.", "B) Genetic differences between students are far greater than supposed.", "C) The advantages of selective schools are too obvious to ignore.", "D) Students' academic performance is determined by their genes."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did a recent research paper claim? 直接索取一项可在原文找到对应陈述的事\n实信息，没有 infer/imply/mainly about 等推断或主旨信号。\n【拆句】主干 a recent research paper claim，是 What 作宾语前置的一般疑问句，结构平铺直\n叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】This is down to \"hereditarian\"(遗传论的) science and a recent paper claimed \"differences in\nexam performance between pupils attending selective and non-selective schools mirror the\ngenetic differences between them\".\n这要归因于「遗传论」科学，一份最近的论文声称「选拔型学校与非选拔型学校学生之间的\n考试成绩差异，反映的是他们之间的基因差异」。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThe type of school students attend makes a difference to their future. 把论文的主\n张换成「学校类型影响前途」，而原文恰恰是说差距并非源于学校类型而是源于基因。\nB) 无中生有\nGenetic differences between students are far greater than supposed. 称「基因差\n异比想象中大得多」，但 P2 只谈差异是否反映基因，并未给出基因差异程度的比较。\nC) 与原文相反\nThe advantages of selective schools are too obvious to ignore. 说选拔型学校优势\n明显，可原文恰好指出 the educational benefits of selective schools largely\ndisappear——优势在控制变量后基本消失。\nD) ✓ 正确\ndifferences in exam performance ... mirror the genetic differences between them ↔\nStudents' academic performance is determined by their genes.\n预判说「学业表现归因于基因」，Students' academic performance is determined\nby their genes. 正是这句话的同义换述，且没有添加额外信息，故锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the author think of the recent research?", "options": ["A) Its result was questionable.", "B) Its implication was positive.", "C) Its influence was rather negligible.", "D) Its conclusions were enlightening."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度题）\n【判型】What does the author think of the recent research? 是「作者怎么看」式态度题，答案\n不在原文事实层，而在作者评价层。\n【拆句】What does the author think of the recent research? 主干 the author think of the\nrecent research，think of 是「对……的看法」，What 提作宾语；句子简单。\n【定位】With such an assertion, the work was predictably greeted by a lot of absurd claims about\n\"genetics determining academic success\". What the research revealed was the rather less\nsurprising result: the educational benefits of selective schools largely disappear once pupils'\ninborn ability and socio-economic background were taken into account. It is a glimpse\nof the blindingly obvious—and there's nothing to back strongly either a hereditary or\nenvironmental argument.\n面对这样的论断，这项研究果然引来一大堆关于「基因决定学业成功」的荒唐说法。而研究\n真正揭示的其实是个相当不令人惊讶的结果：一旦把学生的先天能力和社会经济背景考虑进\n去，选拔型学校的教育优势就大体不复存在。这只是把显而易见的事再说一遍——对遗传论\n和环境论都不构成有力支持。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nnothing to back strongly either a hereditary or environmental argument ↔\nIts result was questionable.\nabsurd claims about \"genetics determining academic success\" ↔ questionable\n预判把作者态度判为质疑/否定，Its result was questionable. 正是这种态度的中性化表\n达，故锁定 A。\nB) 与原文相反\nIts implication was positive. 说研究的启发是正面的，可作者用 absurd nothing to\nback strongly 显然否定，极性完全相反。\nC) 答非所问\nIts influence was rather negligible. 称研究影响微不足道，但原文谈的是研究「结论本\n身可疑」，并未评价研究的社会影响是否微弱。\nD) 与原文相反\nIts conclusions were enlightening. 称研究结论富有启发，恰与作者的 rather less\nsurprising blindingly obvious 相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the author say about the relationship between DNA and intelligence?", "options": ["A) It is one of scientific certainty.", "B) It is not one of cause and effect.", "C) It is subject to interpretation of statistics.", "D) It is not fully examined by gene scientists."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about the relationship between DNA and intelligence? 索\n取一个可在原文锁定的事实判断，是典型细节题。\n【拆句】主干 the author say about the relationship，about 后接 between DNA and\nintelligence 是限定 relationship 的介词短语；结构无陷阱。\n【定位】At best there is a weak statistical association and not a causal link between DNA\nand intelligence.\n充其量也只是 DNA 与智力之间存在一种弱的统计相关，而非因果关系。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nIt is one of scientific certainty. 说两者关系具有科学确定性，但原文用 weak\nstatistical association not a causal link 强调「弱、不确定」；恰是 intimidatory\natmosphere of scientific certainty 批判的对象。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnot a causal link ↔ not one of cause and effect\n预判强调「不是因果」，It is not one of cause and effect. 精确对应定位句，故选 B。\nC) 偷换概念\nIt is subject to interpretation of statistics. 谈的是「这种关系受统计解释影响」，但原\n文判断的是关系本身的性质（弱相关、非因果），不是关系对统计解释的依附程度。\nD) 无中生有\nIt is not fully examined by gene scientists. 说基因科学家尚未充分研究两者关系，可\n原文恰恰说他们用 sophisticated statistics 大力研究，并不缺研究。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do hereditarians need to do to make their claims convincing?", "options": ["A) Take all relevant factors into account in interpreting their data.", "B) Conduct their research using more sophisticated technology.", "C) Gather gene data from people of all social classes.", "D) Cooperate with social scientists in their research."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do hereditarians need to do to make their claims convincing? 索取一条具体做\n法，在原文有对应句即可锁定。\n【拆句】What do hereditarians need to do to make their claims convincing? 主干\nhereditarians need to do 什么，后接 to make their claims convincing 是目的状语；\ntheir 指 hereditarians' 自己的主张，结构清楚。\n【定位】If hereditarians want to advance their cause it will require more balanced interpretation and\nnot just acts of advocacy.\n如果遗传论者想推进他们的事业，就需要更平衡的解读，而不仅仅是搞鼓吹。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nmore balanced interpretation ↔\nTake all relevant factors into account in interpreting their data.\nMedical predictability can rarely be based on DNA alone; the environment matters too.\nSomething as complex as intellect is likely to be affected by many factors beyond genes.\n↔ all relevant factors\n预判要求「全面平衡解读」，Take all relevant factors into account in interpreting\ntheir data. 正是这一要求的具体化，故锁定 A。\nB) 与原文相反\nConduct their research using more sophisticated technology. 让遗传论者用更先进\n的技术，但原文 sophisticated statistics are used to create an intimidatory\natmosphere of scientific certainty 恰恰把「精巧统计」视为问题，而非解药。\nC) 过度推断\nGather gene data from people of all social classes. 让从各阶层采集基因数据，但\n原文 socially defined groups 只是说不能用基因解释社会群体差异，并未建议按阶层\n采样。\nD) 无中生有\nCooperate with social scientists in their research. 让和社会科学家合作，但原文从未\n提及合作研究，社会因素只是被作者用来反驳基因决定论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author warn against in the passage?", "options": ["A) Exaggerating the power of technology in shaping the world.", "B) Losing sight of professional ethics in conducting research.", "C) Misunderstanding the findings of human cognition research.", "D) Promoting discrimination in the name of science."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题）\n【判型】What does the author warn against in the passage? 含 in the passage，要求覆盖整篇\n的核心警示，需把全文行文主线串起来作答。\n【拆句】主干 the author warn against (something) in the passage，warn against 意为「告诫\n不要……」，What 提取被告诫的对象。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nExaggerating the power of technology in shaping the world. 把警示落到「夸大技\n术作用」，但全文核心是基因决定论被包装成科学后用于区别对待人，技术只是末段顺\n带一提。\nB) 无中生有\nLosing sight of professional ethics in conducting research. 称研究者失了职业伦\n理，但原文 power requires a sense of responsibility 谈的是社会层面的权力与责任，\n并未指向研究伦理失守。\nC) 偷换概念\nMisunderstanding the findings of human cognition research. 警示公众误读研究发\n现，但作者警示的是把基因决定论上升为科学并用于歧视，不是公众理解能力问题。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwe must not elevate discrimination to a science ↔\nPromoting discrimination in the name of science.\nallowing people to climb the ladder of life only as far as their cells might suggest ↔\ndiscrimination\n预判强调「警惕用科学包装歧视」，Promoting discrimination in the name of\nscience. 正是同义改写，且与全文主线吻合，故选 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn from Nicola Sturgeon's speech?", "options": ["A) The UK is set to adjust its policy on taxation.", "B) Tourists will have to pay a tax to visit Scotland.", "C) The UK will take new measures to boost tourism.", "D) Edinburgh contributes most to Scotland's tourism."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from Nicola Sturgeon's speech? 直接问从某人讲话里得到何信息，是\n对一个具体事实点的提取。\n【拆句】What do we learn from Nicola Sturgeon's speech? 主干是 What do we learn，from\n引导信息来源，句式平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Scottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors,\nwith Edinburgh likely to lead the way.\n苏格兰各地方议会有望成为英国首批拥有向访客征收费用之权力的议会，爱丁堡很可能率先\n施行。\n【选项】A) 把局部说成整体即以偏概全\n原文只说苏格兰地方议会率先获得对游客征费的权力，并非全国性税制调整。\nB) ✓ 正确\nScottish councils are set to be the first in the UK with the power to levy charges on visitors ↔\nTourists will have to pay a tax to visit Scotland\n预判说苏格兰将率先对游客征费，Tourists will have to pay a tax to visit Scotland 正\n是这件事换一个视角的表达，故锁定 B。\nC) 目的方向反向即与原文相反\n对游客征费的目的是约束过度旅游、补贴地方服务，并非推动旅游业。\nD) 把先行者偷换成最大贡献者\n原文只说爱丁堡 likely to lead the way（率先推行征费），与「对旅游业贡献最大」\n无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "How come the UK has been slow in imposing the tourist tax?", "options": ["A) Its government wants to attract more tourists.", "B) The tax is unlikely to add much to its revenue.", "C) Its ruling party is opposed to taxes and regulation.", "D) It takes time for local governments to reach consensus."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】How come the UK has been slow in imposing the tourist tax? How come 在书面语里\n等同于 Why，问的是一个直接事实性的原因。\n【拆句】How come the UK has been slow in imposing the tourist tax? How come 引导口语化\n提问，等同 Why；slow in doing 是 be slow in 结构，表示在做某事上迟缓。\n【定位】That the UK lags behind is due to our weak, by international standards, local government, as\nwell as the opposition to taxes and regulation of our aggressively pro-market ruling party.\n英国之所以落后，是因为按国际标准衡量我们的地方政府较弱，加之我们激进的亲市场执政\n党反对征税和监管。\n【选项】A) 无中生有：选项内容文中无对应\n原文未谈及英国政府想吸引更多游客这一动机，属无中生有。\nB) 把局限性偷换成原因\n原文说 the amounts raised would be tiny compared with what has been taken\naway by central government since 2010，即征到的钱很少，并不会对财政有大贡\n献；这是「评价征税的局限」，不是英国迟迟不征税的原因。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe opposition to taxes and regulation of our aggressively pro-market ruling party ↔\nIts ruling party is opposed to taxes and regulation\n题干问「原因」，原文给了两条，C 准确复述其中一条（执政党反对税收与监管），与\n预判直接吻合。\nD) 无中生有：杜撰过程\n原文没有谈到「地方政府之间难以达成共识」，只说地方政府总体偏弱、且执政党不\n支持。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Both international and domestic visitors in the UK should pay tourist tax so as to ______.", "options": ["A) Elevate its tourism to international standards", "B) Improve the welfare of its maintenance workers", "C) Promote its cultural exchange with other nations", "D) Ease its financial burden of providing local services"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Both international and domestic visitors in the UK should pay tourist tax so as to\n______. 是不完整陈述句，选项是 so as to 之后的目的状语，本质是问原文给出的目的/\n理由。\n【拆句】Both international and domestic visitors in the UK should pay tourist tax so as to\n______. 主语并列 Both...and...，so as to 引导目的状语，选项是动词原形开头补全空位。\n【定位】There is no reason why visitors to the UK, or domestic tourists on holiday in hotspots\nsuch as Cornwall, should be exempt from taxation-particularly when vital local services\nincluding waste collection, park maintenance and arts and culture spending are under\nunprecedented strain.\n没有理由让英国的国际游客、或在康沃尔等热门地度假的国内游客免于纳税——尤其是在垃\n圾清运、公园维护、文化艺术开支等关键地方服务承受空前压力之时。\n【选项】A) 短语嫁接：取原文短语错嫁宾语\n原文 by international standards 修饰的是 local government 较弱（按国际标准），\n与「把旅游业提升到国际水准」毫无关系；选项偷取原文短语制造干扰。\nB) 对单个词做过度引申即过度推断\n原文只提 park maintenance 是地方服务之一，并未谈及维护工人的福利。\nC) 把内部支出偷换为对外交流\n原文 arts and culture spending 是地方艺术文化开支，而非国际文化交流。\nD) ✓ 正确\nvital local services including waste collection, park maintenance and arts and culture spending\nare under unprecedented strain\n↔ ease its financial burden of providing local services\n预判说「为缓解关键服务承压」，D 把「承压」还原为「财政负担」、把「缓解」还原\n为 ease，是定位句目的成分的标准抽象。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say about Western tourists?", "options": ["A) They don't seem to care about the social cost of tourism.", "B) They don't seem to mind paying for additional services.", "C) They deem travel an important part of their life.", "D) They subject the effects of tourism to scrutiny."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say about Western tourists? 问作者关于 Western tourists 说了\n什么，是对作者评述句的事实提取。\n【拆句】What does the author say about Western tourists? 主干 What does the author say，\n介词 about 引出评述对象，无复杂修饰。\n【定位】Westerners with disposable incomes have often behaved as if they have a right to go wherever\nthey choose with little regard for the consequences.\n有可支配收入的西方人往往表现得仿佛自己有权任意旅行，几乎不顾后果。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nwith little regard for the consequences ↔\nThey don't seem to care about the social cost of tourism\nthe social cost of tourism must also be confronted ↔ the social cost of tourism\ndon't seem to care about the social cost of tourism 把 with little regard for the\nconsequences 的含蓄否定显化，且 consequences=social cost of tourism 由下一句\n锁死，与预判一致。\nB) 无中生有：制造原文未谈的态度\n原文并未提及西方游客是否介意为额外服务付费，话题完全不对。\nC) 把负面评价偷换为中性描述\n原文说他们 behaved as if they have a right to go wherever they choose，强调的是\n「理所当然的态度」而非「把旅行视为生活重要部分」，且与作者批评色彩不符。\nD) 把作者主张说成主体已做即与原文相反\n原文 the social cost of tourism must also be confronted 中的 must 表「应当」，是\n作者主张，而非西方游客已经在做；游客真实状态是 with little regard for the\nconsequences，恰好相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What are UK people's opinions about the levy of tourist tax?", "options": ["A) Supportive.", "B) Skeptical.", "C) Divided.", "D) Unclear."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度题）\n【判型】What are UK people's opinions about the levy of tourist tax? 中 opinions 是态度题的\n标志词，要求对群体整体看法做判断。\n【拆句】What are UK people's opinions about the levy of tourist tax? 主干 What\nare...opinions，about 引出态度对象 the levy of tourist tax（征收旅游税）。\n【选项】A) 态度题常见以偏概全\n支持表态只是 P4-P5 作者及部分城市的一面，businesses are often opposed 直接给\n出反对方，全盘说成 supportive 把局部当作了整体。\nB) 把一端立场扩成全民立场即与原文相反\n原文里同时存在明确支持，并非整体怀疑；P4 作者甚至称之为 bold move，与\nskeptical 方向相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\nbusinesses are often opposed, fearing higher costs will make them uncompetitive ↔ Divided\nThere is no \"one size fits all\" solution to this problem ↔ Divided\n支持方表态与反对方表态在 P6 同段共存，且作者点明 no one-size-fits-all，与预判\n「意见分歧」完全一致，故锁 C。\nD) 区分「分歧」与「不清楚」\n原文立场非常清楚：支持与反对的双方都已明确表态，不存在「看不清」的问题；混淆\n「立场分歧」与「立场不明」。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}]});
